-----\n 183 |     X : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n 184 |         The input data.\n 185 | \n 186 |     allow_nan : bool, default=False\n 187 |         If True, do not throw error when `X` contains NaN.\n 188 | \n 189 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 190 |         The estimator name, used to construct the error message.\n 191 | \n 192 |     input_name : str, default=\"\"\n 193 |         The data name used to construct the error message. In particular\n 194 |         if `input_name` is \"X\" and the data has NaN values and\n 195 |         allow_nan is False, the error message will link to the imputer\n 196 |         documentation.\n 197 | \n 198 |     Examples\n 199 |     --------\n 200 |     >>> from sklearn.utils import assert_all_finite\n 201 |     >>> import numpy as np\n 202 |     >>> array = np.array([1, np.inf, np.nan, 4])\n 203 |     >>> try:\n 204 |     ...     assert_all_finite(array)\n 205 |     ...     print(\"Test passed: Array contains only finite values.\")\n 206 |     ... except ValueError:\n 207 |     ...     print(\"Test failed: Array contains non-finite values.\")\n 208 |     Test failed: Array contains non-finite values.\n 209 |     \"\"\"\n 210 |     _assert_all_finite(\n 211 |         X.data if sp.issparse(X) else X,\n 212 |         allow_nan=allow_nan,\n 213 |         estimator_name=estimator_name,\n 214 |         input_name=input_name,\n 215 |     )\n 216 | \n 217 | \n 218 | def as_float_array(\n 219 |     X, *, copy=True, force_all_finite=\"deprecated\", ensure_all_finite=None\n 220 | ):\n 221 |     \"\"\"Convert an array-like to an array of floats.\n 222 | \n 223 |     The new dtype will be np.float32 or np.float64, depending on the original\n 224 |     type. The function can create a copy or modify the argument depending\n 225 |     on the argument copy.\n 226 | \n 227 |     Parameters\n 228 |     ----------\n 229 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix}\n 230 |         The input data.\n 231 | \n 232 |     copy : bool, default=True\n 233 |         If True, a copy of X will be created. If False, a copy may still be\n 234 |         returned if X's dtype is not a floating point type.\n 235 | \n 236 |     force_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n 237 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in X. The\n 238 |         possibilities are:\n 239 | \n 240 |         - True: Force all values of X to be finite.\n 241 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in X.\n 242 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in X. Values cannot\n 243 |           be infinite.\n 244 | \n 245 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 246 |            ``force_all_finite`` accepts the string ``'allow-nan'``.\n 247 | \n 248 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 249 |            Accepts `pd.NA` and converts it into `np.nan`\n 250 | \n 251 |         .. deprecated:: 1.6\n 252 |            `force_all_finite` was renamed to `ensure_all_finite` and will be removed\n 253 |            in 1.8.\n 254 | \n 255 |     ensure_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n 256 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in X. The\n 257 |         possibilities are:\n 258 | \n 259 |         - True: Force all values of X to be finite.\n 260 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in X.\n 261 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in X. Values cannot\n 262 |           be infinite.\n 263 | \n 264 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n 265 |            `force_all_finite` was renamed to `ensure_all_finite`.\n 266 | \n 267 |     Returns\n 268 |     -------\n 269 |     XT : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n 270 |         An array of type float.\n 271 | \n 272 |     Examples\n 273 |     --------\n 274 |     >>> from sklearn.utils import as_float_array\n 275 |     >>> import numpy as np\n 276 |     >>> array = np.array([0, 0, 1, 2, 2], dtype=np.int64)\n 277 |     >>> as_float_array(array)\n 278 |     array([0., 0., 1., 2., 2.])\n 279 |     \"\"\"\n 280 |     ensure_all_finite = _deprecate_force_all_finite(force_all_finite, ensure_all_finite)\n 281 | \n 282 |     if isinstance(X, np.matrix) or (\n 283 |         not isinstance(X, np.ndarray) and not sp.issparse(X)\n 284 |     ):\n 285 |         return check_array(\n 286 |             X,\n 287 |             accept_sparse=[\"csr\", \"csc\", \"coo\"],\n 288 |             dtype=np.float64,\n 289 |             copy=copy,\n 290 |             ensure_all_finite=ensure_all_finite,\n 291 |             ensure_2d=False,\n 292 |         )\n 293 |     elif sp.issparse(X) and X.dtype in [np.float32, np.float64]:\n 294 |         return X.copy() if copy else X\n 295 |     elif X.dtype in [np.float32, np.float64]:  # is numpy array\n 296 |         return X.copy(\"F\" if X.flags[\"F_CONTIGUOUS\"] else \"C\") if copy else X\n 297 |     else:\n 298 |         if X.dtype.kind in \"uib\" and X.dtype.itemsize <= 4:\n 299 |             return_dtype = np.float32\n 300 |         else:\n 301 |             return_dtype = np.float64\n 302 |         return X.astype(return_dtype)\n 303 | \n 304 | \n 305 | def _is_arraylike(x):\n 306 |     \"\"\"Returns whether the input is array-like.\"\"\"\n 307 |     if sp.issparse(x):\n 308 |         return False\n 309 | \n 310 |     return hasattr(x, \"__len__\") or hasattr(x, \"shape\") or hasattr(x, \"__array__\")\n 311 | \n 312 | \n 313 | def _is_arraylike_not_scalar(array):\n 314 |     \"\"\"Return True if array is array-like and not a scalar\"\"\"\n 315 |     return _is_arraylike(array) and not np.isscalar(array)\n 316 | \n 317 | \n 318 | def _use_interchange_protocol(X):\n 319 |     \"\"\"Use interchange protocol for non-pandas dataframes that follow the protocol.\n 320 | \n 321 |     Note: at this point we chose not to use the interchange API on pandas dataframe\n 322 |     to ensure strict behavioral backward compatibility with older versions of\n 323 |     scikit-learn.\n 324 |     \"\"\"\n 325 |     return not _is_pandas_df(X) and hasattr(X, \"__dataframe__\")\n 326 | \n 327 | \n 328 | def _num_features(X):\n 329 |     \"\"\"Return the number of features in an array-like X.\n 330 | \n 331 |     This helper function tries hard to avoid to materialize an array version\n 332 |     of X unless necessary. For instance, if X is a list of lists,\n 333 |     this function will return the length of the first element, assuming\n 334 |     that subsequent elements are all lists of the same length without\n 335 |     checking.\n 336 |     Parameters\n 337 |     ----------\n 338 |     X : array-like\n 339 |         array-like to get the number of features.\n 340 | \n 341 |     Returns\n 342 |     -------\n 343 |     features : int\n 344 |         Number of features\n 345 |     \"\"\"\n 346 |     type_ = type(X)\n 347 |     if type_.__module__ == \"builtins\":\n 348 |         type_name = type_.__qualname__\n 349 |     else:\n 350 |         type_name = f\"{type_.__module__}.{type_.__qualname__}\"\n 351 |     message = f\"Unable to find the number of features from X of type {type_name}\"\n 352 |     if not hasattr(X, \"__len__\") and not hasattr(X, \"shape\"):\n 353 |         if not hasattr(X, \"__array__\"):\n 354 |             raise TypeError(message)\n 355 |         # Only convert X to a numpy array if there is no cheaper, heuristic\n 356 |         # option.\n 357 |         X = np.asarray(X)\n 358 | \n 359 |     if hasattr(X, \"shape\"):\n 360 |         if not hasattr(X.shape, \"__len__\") or len(X.shape) <= 1:\n 361 |             message += f\" with shape {X.shape}\"\n 362 |             raise TypeError(message)\n 363 |         return X.shape[1]\n 364 | \n 365 |     first_sample = X[0]\n 366 | \n 367 |     # Do not consider an array-like of strings or dicts to be a 2D array\n 368 |     if isinstance(first_sample, (str, bytes, dict)):\n 369 |         message += f\" where the samples are of type {type(first_sample).__qualname__}\"\n 370 |         raise TypeError(message)\n 371 | \n 372 |     try:\n 373 |         # If X is a list of lists, for instance, we assume that all nested\n 374 |         # lists have the same length without checking or converting to\n 375 |         # a numpy array to keep this function call as cheap as possible.\n 376 |         return len(first_sample)\n 377 |     except Exception as err:\n 378 |         raise TypeError(message) from err\n 379 | \n 380 | \n 381 | def _num_samples(x):\n 382 |     \"\"\"Return number of samples in array-like x.\"\"\"\n 383 |     message = \"Expected sequence or array-like, got %s\" % type(x)\n 384 |     if hasattr(x, \"fit\") and callable(x.fit):\n 385 |         # Don't get num_samples from an ensembles length!\n 386 |         raise TypeError(message)\n 387 | \n 388 |     if _use_interchange_protocol(x):\n 389 |         return x.__dataframe__().num_rows()\n 390 | \n 391 |     if not hasattr(x, \"__len__\") and not hasattr(x, \"shape\"):\n 392 |         if hasattr(x, \"__array__\"):\n 393 |             x = np.asarray(x)\n 394 |         else:\n 395 |             raise TypeError(message)\n 396 | \n 397 |     if hasattr(x, \"shape\") and x.shape is not None:\n 398 |         if len(x.shape) == 0:\n 399 |             raise TypeError(\n 400 |                 \"Singleton array %r cannot be considered a valid collection.\" % x\n 401 |             )\n 402 |         # Check that shape is returning an integer or default to len\n 403 |         # Dask dataframes may not return numeric shape[0] value\n 404 |         if isinstance(x.shape[0], numbers.Integral):\n 405 |             return x.shape[0]\n 406 | \n 407 |     try:\n 408 |         return len(x)\n 409 |     except TypeError as type_error:\n 410 |         raise TypeError(message) from type_error\n 411 | \n 412 | \n 413 | def check_memory(memory):\n 414 |     \"\"\"Check that ``memory`` is joblib.Memory-like.\n 415 | \n 416 |     joblib.Memory-like means that ``memory`` can be converted into a\n 417 |     joblib.Memory instance (typically a str denoting the ``location``)\n 418 |     or has the same interface (has a ``cache`` method).\n 419 | \n 420 |     Parameters\n 421 |     ----------\n 422 |     memory : None, str or object with the joblib.Memory interface\n 423 |         - If string, the location where to create the `joblib.Memory` interface.\n 424 |         - If None, no caching is done and the Memory object is completely transparent.\n 425 | \n 426 |     Returns\n 427 |     -------\n 428 |     memory : object with the joblib.Memory interface\n 429 |         A correct joblib.Memory object.\n 430 | \n 431 |     Raises\n 432 |     ------\n 433 |     ValueError\n 434 |         If ``memory`` is not joblib.Memory-like.\n 435 | \n 436 |     Examples\n 437 |     --------\n 438 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_memory\n 439 |     >>> check_memory(\"caching_dir\")\n 440 |     Memory(location=caching_dir/joblib)\n 441 |     \"\"\"\n 442 |     if memory is None or isinstance(memory, str):\n 443 |         memory = joblib.Memory(location=memory, verbose=0)\n 444 |     elif not hasattr(memory, \"cache\"):\n 445 |         raise ValueError(\n 446 |             \"'memory' should be None, a string or have the same\"\n 447 |             \" interface as joblib.Memory.\"\n 448 |             \" Got memory='{}' instead.\".format(memory)\n 449 |         )\n 450 |     return memory\n 451 | \n 452 | \n 453 | def check_consistent_length(*arrays):\n 454 |     \"\"\"Check that all arrays have consistent first dimensions.\n 455 | \n 456 |     Checks whether all objects in arrays have the same shape or length.\n 457 | \n 458 |     Parameters\n 459 |     ----------\n 460 |     *arrays : list or tuple of input objects.\n 461 |         Objects that will be checked for consistent length.\n 462 | \n 463 |     Examples\n 464 |     --------\n 465 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_consistent_length\n 466 |     >>> a = [1, 2, 3]\n 467 |     >>> b = [2, 3, 4]\n 468 |     >>> check_consistent_length(a, b)\n 469 |     \"\"\"\n 470 | \n 471 |     lengths = [_num_samples(X) for X in arrays if X is not None]\n 472 |     uniques = np.unique(lengths)\n 473 |     if len(uniques) > 1:\n 474 |         raise ValueError(\n 475 |             \"Found input variables with inconsistent numbers of samples: %r\"\n 476 |             % [int(l) for l in lengths]\n 477 |         )\n 478 | \n 479 | \n 480 | def _make_indexable(iterable):\n 481 |     \"\"\"Ensure iterable supports indexing or convert to an indexable variant.\n 482 | \n 483 |     Convert sparse matrices to csr and other non-indexable iterable to arrays.\n 484 |     Let `None` and indexable objects (e.g. pandas dataframes) pass unchanged.\n 485 | \n 486 |     Parameters\n 487 |     ----------\n 488 |     iterable : {list, dataframe, ndarray, sparse matrix} or None\n 489 |         Object to be converted to an indexable iterable.\n 490 |     \"\"\"\n 491 |     if sp.issparse(iterable):\n 492 |         return iterable.tocsr()\n 493 |     elif hasattr(iterable, \"__getitem__\") or hasattr(iterable, \"iloc\"):\n 494 |         return iterable\n 495 |     elif iterable is None:\n 496 |         return iterable\n 497 |     return np.array(iterable)\n 498 | \n 499 | \n 500 | def indexable(*iterables):\n 501 |     \"\"\"Make arrays indexable for cross-validation.\n 502 | \n 503 |     Checks consistent length, passes through None, and ensures that everything\n 504 |     can be indexed by converting sparse matrices to csr and converting\n 505 |     non-iterable objects to arrays.\n 506 | \n 507 |     Parameters\n 508 |     ----------\n 509 |     *iterables : {lists, dataframes, ndarrays, sparse matrices}\n 510 |         List of objects to ensure sliceability.\n 511 | \n 512 |     Returns\n 513 |     -------\n 514 |     result : list of {ndarray, sparse matrix, dataframe} or None\n 515 |         Returns a list containing indexable arrays (i.e. NumPy array,\n 516 |         sparse matrix, or dataframe) or `None`.\n 517 | \n 518 |     Examples\n 519 |     --------\n 520 |     >>> from sklearn.utils import indexable\n 521 |     >>> from scipy.sparse import csr_matrix\n 522 |     >>> import numpy as np\n 523 |     >>> iterables = [\n 524 |     ...     [1, 2, 3], np.array([2, 3, 4]), None, csr_matrix([[5], [6], [7]])\n 525 |     ... ]\n 526 |     >>> indexable(*iterables)\n 527 |     [[1, 2, 3], array([2, 3, 4]), None, <...Sparse...dtype 'int64'...shape (3, 1)>]\n 528 |     \"\"\"\n 529 | \n 530 |     result = [_make_indexable(X) for X in iterables]\n 531 |     check_consistent_length(*result)\n 532 |     return result\n 533 | \n 534 | \n 535 | def _ensure_sparse_format(\n 536 |     sparse_container,\n 537 |     accept_sparse,\n 538 |     dtype,\n 539 |     copy,\n 540 |     ensure_all_finite,\n 541 |     accept_large_sparse,\n 542 |     estimator_name=None,\n 543 |     input_name=\"\",\n 544 | ):\n 545 |     \"\"\"Convert a sparse container to a given format.\n 546 | \n 547 |     Checks the sparse format of `sparse_container` and converts if necessary.\n 548 | \n 549 |     Parameters\n 550 |     ----------\n 551 |     sparse_container : sparse matrix or array\n 552 |         Input to validate and convert.\n 553 | \n 554 |     accept_sparse : str, bool or list/tuple of str\n 555 |         String[s] representing allowed sparse matrix formats ('csc',\n 556 |         'csr', 'coo', 'dok', 'bsr', 'lil', 'dia'). If the input is sparse but\n 557 |         not in the allowed format, it will be converted to the first listed\n 558 |         format. True allows the input to be any format. False means\n 559 |         that a sparse matrix input will raise an error.\n 560 | \n 561 |     dtype : str, type or None\n 562 |         Data type of result. If None, the dtype of the input is preserved.\n 563 | \n 564 |     copy : bool\n 565 |         Whether a forced copy will be triggered. If copy=False, a copy might\n 566 |         be triggered by a conversion.\n 567 | \n 568 |     ensure_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan'\n 569 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in X. The\n 570 |         possibilities are:\n 571 | \n 572 |         - True: Force all values of X to be finite.\n 573 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in X.\n 574 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in X. Values cannot\n 575 |           be infinite.\n 576 | \n 577 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 578 |            ``ensure_all_finite`` accepts the string ``'allow-nan'``.\n 579 | \n 580 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 581 |            Accepts `pd.NA` and converts it into `np.nan`\n 582 | \n 583 | \n 584 |     estimator_name : str, default=None\n 585 |         The estimator name, used to construct the error message.\n 586 | \n 587 |     input_name : str, default=\"\"\n 588 |         The data name used to construct the error message. In particular\n 589 |         if `input_name` is \"X\" and the data has NaN values and\n 590 |         allow_nan is False, the error message will link to the imputer\n 591 |         documentation.\n 592 | \n 593 |     Returns\n 594 |     -------\n 595 |     sparse_container_converted : sparse matrix or array\n 596 |         Sparse container (matrix/array) that is ensured to have an allowed type.\n 597 |     \"\"\"\n 598 |     if dtype is None:\n 599 |         dtype = sparse_container.dtype\n 600 | \n 601 |     changed_format = False\n 602 |     sparse_container_type_name = type(sparse_container).__name__\n 603 | \n 604 |     if isinstance(accept_sparse, str):\n 605 |         accept_sparse = [accept_sparse]\n 606 | \n 607 |     # Indices dtype validation\n 608 |     _check_large_sparse(sparse_container, accept_large_sparse)\n 609 | \n 610 |     if accept_sparse is False:\n 611 |         padded_input = \" for \" + input_name if input_name else \"\"\n 612 |         raise TypeError(\n 613 |             f\"Sparse data was passed{padded_input}, but dense data is required. \"\n 614 |             \"Use '.toarray()' to convert to a dense numpy array.\"\n 615 |         )\n 616 |     elif isinstance(accept_sparse, (list, tuple)):\n 617 |         if len(accept_sparse) == 0:\n 618 |             raise ValueError(\n 619 |                 \"When providing 'accept_sparse' as a tuple or list, it must contain at \"\n 620 |                 \"least one string value.\"\n 621 |             )\n 622 |         # ensure correct sparse format\n 623 |         if sparse_container.format not in accept_sparse:\n 624 |             # create new with correct sparse\n 625 |             sparse_container = sparse_container.asformat(accept_sparse[0])\n 626 |             changed_format = True\n 627 |     elif accept_sparse is not True:\n 628 |         # any other type\n 629 |         raise ValueError(\n 630 |             \"Parameter 'accept_sparse' should be a string, boolean or list of strings.\"\n 631 |             f\" You provided 'accept_sparse={accept_sparse}'.\"\n 632 |         )\n 633 | \n 634 |     if dtype != sparse_container.dtype:\n 635 |         # convert dtype\n 636 |         sparse_container = sparse_container.astype(dtype)\n 637 |     elif copy and not changed_format:\n 638 |         # force copy\n 639 |         sparse_container = sparse_container.copy()\n 640 | \n 641 |     if ensure_all_finite:\n 642 |         if not hasattr(sparse_container, \"data\"):\n 643 |             warnings.warn(\n 644 |                 f\"Can't check {sparse_container.format} sparse matrix for nan or inf.\",\n 645 |                 stacklevel=2,\n 646 |             )\n 647 |         else:\n 648 |             _assert_all_finite(\n 649 |                 sparse_container.data,\n 650 |                 allow_nan=ensure_all_finite == \"allow-nan\",\n 651 |                 estimator_name=estimator_name,\n 652 |                 input_name=input_name,\n 653 |             )\n 654 | \n 655 |     # TODO: Remove when the minimum version of SciPy supported is 1.12\n 656 |     # With SciPy sparse arrays, conversion from DIA format to COO, CSR, or BSR\n 657 |     # triggers the use of `np.int64` indices even if the data is such that it could\n 658 |     # be more efficiently represented with `np.int32` indices.\n 659 |     # https://github.com/scipy/scipy/issues/19245 Since not all scikit-learn\n 660 |     # algorithms support large indices, the following code downcasts to `np.int32`\n 661 |     # indices when it's safe to do so.\n 662 |     if changed_format:\n 663 |         # accept_sparse is specified to a specific format and a conversion occurred\n 664 |         requested_sparse_format = accept_sparse[0]\n 665 |         _preserve_dia_indices_dtype(\n 666 |             sparse_container, sparse_container_type_name, requested_sparse_format\n 667 |         )\n 668 | \n 669 |     return sparse_container\n 670 | \n 671 | \n 672 | def _ensure_no_complex_data(array):\n 673 |     if (\n 674 |         hasattr(array, \"dtype\")\n 675 |         and array.dtype is not None\n 676 |         and hasattr(array.dtype, \"kind\")\n 677 |         and array.dtype.kind == \"c\"\n 678 |     ):\n 679 |         raise ValueError(\"Complex data not supported\\n{}\\n\".format(array))\n 680 | \n 681 | \n 682 | def _check_estimator_name(estimator):\n 683 |     if estimator is not None:\n 684 |         if isinstance(estimator, str):\n 685 |             return estimator\n 686 |         else:\n 687 |             return estimator.__class__.__name__\n 688 |     return None\n 689 | \n 690 | \n 691 | def _pandas_dtype_needs_early_conversion(pd_dtype):\n 692 |     \"\"\"Return True if pandas extension pd_dtype need to be converted early.\"\"\"\n 693 |     # Check these early for pandas versions without extension dtypes\n 694 |     from pandas import SparseDtype\n 695 |     from pandas.api.types import (\n 696 |         is_bool_dtype,\n 697 |         is_float_dtype,\n 698 |         is_integer_dtype,\n 699 |     )\n 700 | \n 701 |     if is_bool_dtype(pd_dtype):\n 702 |         # bool and extension booleans need early conversion because __array__\n 703 |         # converts mixed dtype dataframes into object dtypes\n 704 |         return True\n 705 | \n 706 |     if isinstance(pd_dtype, SparseDtype):\n 707 |         # Sparse arrays will be converted later in `check_array`\n 708 |         return False\n 709 | \n 710 |     try:\n 711 |         from pandas.api.types import is_extension_array_dtype\n 712 |     except ImportError:\n 713 |         return False\n 714 | \n 715 |     if isinstance(pd_dtype, SparseDtype) or not is_extension_array_dtype(pd_dtype):\n 716 |         # Sparse arrays will be converted later in `check_array`\n 717 |         # Only handle extension arrays for integer and floats\n 718 |         return False\n 719 |     elif is_float_dtype(pd_dtype):\n 720 |         # Float ndarrays can normally support nans. They need to be converted\n 721 |         # first to map pd.NA to np.nan\n 722 |         return True\n 723 |     elif is_integer_dtype(pd_dtype):\n 724 |         # XXX: Warn when converting from a high integer to a float\n 725 |         return True\n 726 | \n 727 |     return False\n 728 | \n 729 | \n 730 | def _is_extension_array_dtype(array):\n 731 |     # Pandas extension arrays have a dtype with an na_value\n 732 |     return hasattr(array, \"dtype\") and hasattr(array.dtype, \"na_value\")\n 733 | \n 734 | \n 735 | def check_array(\n 736 |     array,\n 737 |     accept_sparse=False,\n 738 |     *,\n 739 |     accept_large_sparse=True,\n 740 |     dtype=\"numeric\",\n 741 |     order=None,\n 742 |     copy=False,\n 743 |     force_writeable=False,\n 744 |     force_all_finite=\"deprecated\",\n 745 |     ensure_all_finite=None,\n 746 |     ensure_non_negative=False,\n 747 |     ensure_2d=True,\n 748 |     allow_nd=False,\n 749 |     ensure_min_samples=1,\n 750 |     ensure_min_features=1,\n 751 |     estimator=None,\n 752 |     input_name=\"\",\n 753 | ):\n 754 |     \"\"\"Input validation on an array, list, sparse matrix or similar.\n 755 | \n 756 |     By default, the input is checked to be a non-empty 2D array containing\n 757 |     only finite values. If the dtype of the array is object, attempt\n 758 |     converting to float, raising on failure.\n 759 | \n 760 |     Parameters\n 761 |     ----------\n 762 |     array : object\n 763 |         Input object to check / convert.\n 764 | \n 765 |     accept_sparse : str, bool or list/tuple of str, default=False\n 766 |         String[s] representing allowed sparse matrix formats, such as 'csc',\n 767 |         'csr', etc. If the input is sparse but not in the allowed format,\n 768 |         it will be converted to the first listed format. True allows the input\n 769 |         to be any format. False means that a sparse matrix input will\n 770 |         raise an error.\n 771 | \n 772 |     accept_large_sparse : bool, default=True\n 773 |         If a CSR, CSC, COO or BSR sparse matrix is supplied and accepted by\n 774 |         accept_sparse, accept_large_sparse=False will cause it to be accepted\n 775 |         only if its indices are stored with a 32-bit dtype.\n 776 | \n 777 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 778 | \n 779 |     dtype : 'numeric', type, list of type or None, default='numeric'\n 780 |         Data type of result. If None, the dtype of the input is preserved.\n 781 |         If \"numeric\", dtype is preserved unless array.dtype is object.\n 782 |         If dtype is a list of types, conversion on the first type is only\n 783 |         performed if the dtype of the input is not in the list.\n 784 | \n 785 |     order : {'F', 'C'} or None, default=None\n 786 |         Whether an array will be forced to be fortran or c-style.\n 787 |         When order is None (default), then if copy=False, nothing is ensured\n 788 |         about the memory layout of the output array; otherwise (copy=True)\n 789 |         the memory layout of the returned array is kept as close as possible\n 790 |         to the original array.\n 791 | \n 792 |     copy : bool, default=False\n 793 |         Whether a forced copy will be triggered. If copy=False, a copy might\n 794 |         be triggered by a conversion.\n 795 | \n 796 |     force_writeable : bool, default=False\n 797 |         Whether to force the output array to be writeable. If True, the returned array\n 798 |         is guaranteed to be writeable, which may require a copy. Otherwise the\n 799 |         writeability of the input array is preserved.\n 800 | \n 801 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n 802 | \n 803 |     force_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n 804 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array. The\n 805 |         possibilities are:\n 806 | \n 807 |         - True: Force all values of array to be finite.\n 808 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array.\n 809 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in array. Values\n 810 |           cannot be infinite.\n 811 | \n 812 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n 813 |            ``force_all_finite`` accepts the string ``'allow-nan'``.\n 814 | \n 815 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 816 |            Accepts `pd.NA` and converts it into `np.nan`\n 817 | \n 818 |         .. deprecated:: 1.6\n 819 |            `force_all_finite` was renamed to `ensure_all_finite` and will be removed\n 820 |            in 1.8.\n 821 | \n 822 |     ensure_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n 823 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array. The\n 824 |         possibilities are:\n 825 | \n 826 |         - True: Force all values of array to be finite.\n 827 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array.\n 828 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan and pd.NA values in array. Values\n 829 |           cannot be infinite.\n 830 | \n 831 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n 832 |            `force_all_finite` was renamed to `ensure_all_finite`.\n 833 | \n 834 |     ensure_non_negative : bool, default=False\n 835 |         Make sure the array has only non-negative values. If True, an array that\n 836 |         contains negative values will raise a ValueError.\n 837 | \n 838 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n 839 | \n 840 |     ensure_2d : bool, default=True\n 841 |         Whether to raise a value error if array is not 2D.\n 842 | \n 843 |     allow_nd : bool, default=False\n 844 |         Whether to allow array.ndim > 2.\n 845 | \n 846 |     ensure_min_samples : int, default=1\n 847 |         Make sure that the array has a minimum number of samples in its first\n 848 |         axis (rows for a 2D array). Setting to 0 disables this check.\n 849 | \n 850 |     ensure_min_features : int, default=1\n 851 |         Make sure that the 2D array has some minimum number of features\n 852 |         (columns). The default value of 1 rejects empty datasets.\n 853 |         This check is only enforced when the input data has effectively 2\n 854 |         dimensions or is originally 1D and ``ensure_2d`` is True. Setting to 0\n 855 |         disables this check.\n 856 | \n 857 |     estimator : str or estimator instance, default=None\n 858 |         If passed, include the name of the estimator in warning messages.\n 859 | \n 860 |     input_name : str, default=\"\"\n 861 |         The data name used to construct the error message. In particular\n 862 |         if `input_name` is \"X\" and the data has NaN values and\n 863 |         allow_nan is False, the error message will link to the imputer\n 864 |         documentation.\n 865 | \n 866 |         .. versionadded:: 1.1.0\n 867 | \n 868 |     Returns\n 869 |     -------\n 870 |     array_converted : object\n 871 |         The converted and validated array.\n 872 | \n 873 |     Examples\n 874 |     --------\n 875 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_array\n 876 |     >>> X = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]\n 877 |     >>> X_checked = check_array(X)\n 878 |     >>> X_checked\n 879 |     array([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])\n 880 |     \"\"\"\n 881 |     ensure_all_finite = _deprecate_force_all_finite(force_all_finite, ensure_all_finite)\n 882 | \n 883 |     if isinstance(array, np.matrix):\n 884 |         raise TypeError(\n 885 |             \"np.matrix is not supported. Please convert to a numpy array with \"\n 886 |             \"np.asarray. For more information see: \"\n 887 |             \"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/reference/generated/numpy.matrix.html\"\n 888 |         )\n 889 | \n 890 |     xp, is_array_api_compliant = get_namespace(array)\n 891 | \n 892 |     # store reference to original array to check if copy is needed when\n 893 |     # function returns\n 894 |     array_orig = array\n 895 | \n 896 |     # store whether originally we wanted numeric dtype\n 897 |     dtype_numeric = isinstance(dtype, str) and dtype == \"numeric\"\n 898 | \n 899 |     dtype_orig = getattr(array, \"dtype\", None)\n 900 |     if not is_array_api_compliant and not hasattr(dtype_orig, \"kind\"):\n 901 |         # not a data type (e.g. a column named dtype in a pandas DataFrame)\n 902 |         dtype_orig = None\n 903 | \n 904 |     # check if the object contains several dtypes (typically a pandas\n 905 |     # DataFrame), and store them. If not, store None.\n 906 |     dtypes_orig = None\n 907 |     pandas_requires_conversion = False\n 908 |     # track if we have a Series-like object to raise a better error message\n 909 |     type_if_series = None\n 910 |     if hasattr(array, \"dtypes\") and hasattr(array.dtypes, \"__array__\"):\n 911 |         # throw warning if columns are sparse. If all columns are sparse, then\n 912 |         # array.sparse exists and sparsity will be preserved (later).\n 913 |         with suppress(ImportError):\n 914 |             from pandas import SparseDtype\n 915 | \n 916 |             def is_sparse(dtype):\n 917 |                 return isinstance(dtype, SparseDtype)\n 918 | \n 919 |             if not hasattr(array, \"sparse\") and array.dtypes.apply(is_sparse).any():\n 920 |                 warnings.warn(\n 921 |                     \"pandas.DataFrame with sparse columns found.\"\n 922 |                     \"It will be converted to a dense numpy array.\"\n 923 |                 )\n 924 | \n 925 |         dtypes_orig = list(array.dtypes)\n 926 |         pandas_requires_conversion = any(\n 927 |             _pandas_dtype_needs_early_conversion(i) for i in dtypes_orig\n 928 |         )\n 929 |         if all(isinstance(dtype_iter, np.dtype) for dtype_iter in dtypes_orig):\n 930 |             dtype_orig = np.result_type(*dtypes_orig)\n 931 |         elif pandas_requires_conversion and any(d == object for d in dtypes_orig):\n 932 |             # Force object if any of the dtypes is an object\n 933 |             dtype_orig = object\n 934 | \n 935 |     elif (_is_extension_array_dtype(array) or hasattr(array, \"iloc\")) and hasattr(\n 936 |         array, \"dtype\"\n 937 |     ):\n 938 |         # array is a pandas series\n 939 |         type_if_series = type(array)\n 940 |         pandas_requires_conversion = _pandas_dtype_needs_early_conversion(array.dtype)\n 941 |         if isinstance(array.dtype, np.dtype):\n 942 |             dtype_orig = array.dtype\n 943 |         else:\n 944 |             # Set to None to let array.astype work out the best dtype\n 945 |             dtype_orig = None\n 946 | \n 947 |     if dtype_numeric:\n 948 |         if (\n 949 |             dtype_orig is not None\n 950 |             and hasattr(dtype_orig, \"kind\")\n 951 |             and dtype_orig.kind == \"O\"\n 952 |         ):\n 953 |             # if input is object, convert to float.\n 954 |             dtype = xp.float64\n 955 |         else:\n 956 |             dtype = None\n 957 | \n 958 |     if isinstance(dtype, (list, tuple)):\n 959 |         if dtype_orig is not None and dtype_orig in dtype:\n 960 |             # no dtype conversion required\n 961 |             dtype = None\n 962 |         else:\n 963 |             # dtype conversion required. Let's select the first element of the\n 964 |             # list of accepted types.\n 965 |             dtype = dtype[0]\n 966 | \n 967 |     if pandas_requires_conversion:\n 968 |         # pandas dataframe requires conversion earlier to handle extension dtypes with\n 969 |         # nans\n 970 |         # Use the original dtype for conversion if dtype is None\n 971 |         new_dtype = dtype_orig if dtype is None else dtype\n 972 |         array = array.astype(new_dtype)\n 973 |         # Since we converted here, we do not need to convert again later\n 974 |         dtype = None\n 975 | \n 976 |     if ensure_all_finite not in (True, False, \"allow-nan\"):\n 977 |         raise ValueError(\n 978 |             \"ensure_all_finite should be a bool or 'allow-nan'. Got \"\n 979 |             f\"{ensure_all_finite!r} instead.\"\n 980 |         )\n 981 | \n 982 |     if dtype is not None and _is_numpy_namespace(xp):\n 983 |         # convert to dtype object to conform to Array API to be use `xp.isdtype` later\n 984 |         dtype = np.dtype(dtype)\n 985 | \n 986 |     estimator_name = _check_estimator_name(estimator)\n 987 |     context = \" by %s\" % estimator_name if estimator is not None else \"\"\n 988 | \n 989 |     # When all dataframe columns are sparse, convert to a sparse array\n 990 |     if hasattr(array, \"sparse\") and array.ndim > 1:\n 991 |         with suppress(ImportError):\n 992 |             from pandas import SparseDtype  # noqa: F811\n 993 | \n 994 |             def is_sparse(dtype):\n 995 |                 return isinstance(dtype, SparseDtype)\n 996 | \n 997 |             if array.dtypes.apply(is_sparse).all():\n 998 |                 # DataFrame.sparse only supports `to_coo`\n 999 |                 array = array.sparse.to_coo()\n1000 |                 if array.dtype == np.dtype(\"object\"):\n1001 |                     unique_dtypes = set([dt.subtype.name for dt in array_orig.dtypes])\n1002 |                     if len(unique_dtypes) > 1:\n1003 |                         raise ValueError(\n1004 |                             \"Pandas DataFrame with mixed sparse extension arrays \"\n1005 |                             \"generated a sparse matrix with object dtype which \"\n1006 |                             \"can not be converted to a scipy sparse matrix.\"\n1007 |                             \"Sparse extension arrays should all have the same \"\n1008 |                             \"numeric type.\"\n1009 |                         )\n1010 | \n1011 |     if sp.issparse(array):\n1012 |         _ensure_no_complex_data(array)\n1013 |         array = _ensure_sparse_format(\n1014 |             array,\n1015 |             accept_sparse=accept_sparse,\n1016 |             dtype=dtype,\n1017 |             copy=copy,\n1018 |             ensure_all_finite=ensure_all_finite,\n1019 |             accept_large_sparse=accept_large_sparse,\n1020 |             estimator_name=estimator_name,\n1021 |             input_name=input_name,\n1022 |         )\n1023 |         if ensure_2d and array.ndim < 2:\n1024 |             raise ValueError(\n1025 |                 f\"Expected 2D input, got input with shape {array.shape}.\\n\"\n1026 |                 \"Reshape your data either using array.reshape(-1, 1) if \"\n1027 |                 \"your data has a single feature or array.reshape(1, -1) \"\n1028 |                 \"if it contains a single sample.\"\n1029 |             )\n1030 |     else:\n1031 |         # If np.array(..) gives ComplexWarning, then we convert the warning\n1032 |         # to an error. This is needed because specifying a non complex\n1033 |         # dtype to the function converts complex to real dtype,\n1034 |         # thereby passing the test made in the lines following the scope\n1035 |         # of warnings context manager.\n1036 |         with warnings.catch_warnings():\n1037 |             try:\n1038 |                 warnings.simplefilter(\"error\", ComplexWarning)\n1039 |                 if dtype is not None and xp.isdtype(dtype, \"integral\"):\n1040 |                     # Conversion float -> int should not contain NaN or\n1041 |                     # inf (numpy#14412). We cannot use casting='safe' because\n1042 |                     # then conversion float -> int would be disallowed.\n1043 |                     array = _asarray_with_order(array, order=order, xp=xp)\n1044 |                     if xp.isdtype(array.dtype, (\"real floating\", \"complex floating\")):\n1045 |                         _assert_all_finite(\n1046 |                             array,\n1047 |                             allow_nan=False,\n1048 |                             msg_dtype=dtype,\n1049 |                             estimator_name=estimator_name,\n1050 |                             input_name=input_name,\n1051 |                         )\n1052 |                     array = xp.astype(array, dtype, copy=False)\n1053 |                 else:\n1054 |                     array = _asarray_with_order(array, order=order, dtype=dtype, xp=xp)\n1055 |             except ComplexWarning as complex_warning:\n1056 |                 raise ValueError(\n1057 |                     \"Complex data not supported\\n{}\\n\".format(array)\n1058 |                 ) from complex_warning\n1059 | \n1060 |         # It is possible that the np.array(..) gave no warning. This happens\n1061 |         # when no dtype conversion happened, for example dtype = None. The\n1062 |         # result is that np.array(..) produces an array of complex dtype\n1063 |         # and we need to catch and raise exception for such cases.\n1064 |         _ensure_no_complex_data(array)\n1065 | \n1066 |         if ensure_2d:\n1067 |             # If input is scalar raise error\n1068 |             if array.ndim == 0:\n1069 |                 raise ValueError(\n1070 |                     \"Expected 2D array, got scalar array instead:\\narray={}.\\n\"\n1071 |                     \"Reshape your data either using array.reshape(-1, 1) if \"\n1072 |                     \"your data has a single feature or array.reshape(1, -1) \"\n1073 |                     \"if it contains a single sample.\".format(array)\n1074 |                 )\n1075 |             # If input is 1D raise error\n1076 |             if array.ndim == 1:\n1077 |                 # If input is a Series-like object (eg. pandas Series or polars Series)\n1078 |                 if type_if_series is not None:\n1079 |                     msg = (\n1080 |                         f\"Expected a 2-dimensional container but got {type_if_series} \"\n1081 |                         \"instead. Pass a DataFrame containing a single row (i.e. \"\n1082 |                         \"single sample) or a single column (i.e. single feature) \"\n1083 |                         \"instead.\"\n1084 |                     )\n1085 |                 else:\n1086 |                     msg = (\n1087 |                         f\"Expected 2D array, got 1D array instead:\\narray={array}.\\n\"\n1088 |                         \"Reshape your data either using array.reshape(-1, 1) if \"\n1089 |                         \"your data has a single feature or array.reshape(1, -1) \"\n1090 |                         \"if it contains a single sample.\"\n1091 |                     )\n1092 |                 raise ValueError(msg)\n1093 | \n1094 |         if dtype_numeric and hasattr(array.dtype, \"kind\") and array.dtype.kind in \"USV\":\n1095 |             raise ValueError(\n1096 |                 \"dtype='numeric' is not compatible with arrays of bytes/strings.\"\n1097 |                 \"Convert your data to numeric values explicitly instead.\"\n1098 |             )\n1099 |         if not allow_nd and array.ndim >= 3:\n1100 |             raise ValueError(\n1101 |                 \"Found array with dim %d. %s expected <= 2.\"\n1102 |                 % (array.ndim, estimator_name)\n1103 |             )\n1104 | \n1105 |         if ensure_all_finite:\n1106 |             _assert_all_finite(\n1107 |                 array,\n1108 |                 input_name=input_name,\n1109 |                 estimator_name=estimator_name,\n1110 |                 allow_nan=ensure_all_finite == \"allow-nan\",\n1111 |             )\n1112 | \n1113 |         if copy:\n1114 |             if _is_numpy_namespace(xp):\n1115 |                 # only make a copy if `array` and `array_orig` may share memory`\n1116 |                 if np.may_share_memory(array, array_orig):\n1117 |                     array = _asarray_with_order(\n1118 |                         array, dtype=dtype, order=order, copy=True, xp=xp\n1119 |                     )\n1120 |             else:\n1121 |                 # always make a copy for non-numpy arrays\n1122 |                 array = _asarray_with_order(\n1123 |                     array, dtype=dtype, order=order, copy=True, xp=xp\n1124 |                 )\n1125 | \n1126 |     if ensure_min_samples > 0:\n1127 |         n_samples = _num_samples(array)\n1128 |         if n_samples < ensure_min_samples:\n1129 |             raise ValueError(\n1130 |                 \"Found array with %d sample(s) (shape=%s) while a\"\n1131 |                 \" minimum of %d is required%s.\"\n1132 |                 % (n_samples, array.shape, ensure_min_samples, context)\n1133 |             )\n1134 | \n1135 |     if ensure_min_features > 0 and array.ndim == 2:\n1136 |         n_features = array.shape[1]\n1137 |         if n_features < ensure_min_features:\n1138 |             raise ValueError(\n1139 |                 \"Found array with %d feature(s) (shape=%s) while\"\n1140 |                 \" a minimum of %d is required%s.\"\n1141 |                 % (n_features, array.shape, ensure_min_features, context)\n1142 |             )\n1143 | \n1144 |     if ensure_non_negative:\n1145 |         whom = input_name\n1146 |         if estimator_name:\n1147 |             whom += f\" in {estimator_name}\"\n1148 |         check_non_negative(array, whom)\n1149 | \n1150 |     if force_writeable:\n1151 |         # By default, array.copy() creates a C-ordered copy. We set order=K to\n1152 |         # preserve the order of the array.\n1153 |         copy_params = {\"order\": \"K\"} if not sp.issparse(array) else {}\n1154 | \n1155 |         array_data = array.data if sp.issparse(array) else array\n1156 |         flags = getattr(array_data, \"flags\", None)\n1157 |         if not getattr(flags, \"writeable\", True):\n1158 |             # This situation can only happen when copy=False, the array is read-only and\n1159 |             # a writeable output is requested. This is an ambiguous setting so we chose\n1160 |             # to always (except for one specific setting, see below) make a copy to\n1161 |             # ensure that the output is writeable, even if avoidable, to not overwrite\n1162 |             # the user's data by surprise.\n1163 | \n1164 |             if _is_pandas_df_or_series(array_orig):\n1165 |                 try:\n1166 |                     # In pandas >= 3, np.asarray(df), called earlier in check_array,\n1167 |                     # returns a read-only intermediate array. It can be made writeable\n1168 |                     # safely without copy because if the original DataFrame was backed\n1169 |                     # by a read-only array, trying to change the flag would raise an\n1170 |                     # error, in which case we make a copy.\n1171 |                     array_data.flags.writeable = True\n1172 |                 except ValueError:\n1173 |                     array = array.copy(**copy_params)\n1174 |             else:\n1175 |                 array = array.copy(**copy_params)\n1176 | \n1177 |     return array\n1178 | \n1179 | \n1180 | def _check_large_sparse(X, accept_large_sparse=False):\n1181 |     \"\"\"Raise a ValueError if X has 64bit indices and accept_large_sparse=False\"\"\"\n1182 |     if not accept_large_sparse:\n1183 |         supported_indices = [\"int32\"]\n1184 |         if X.format == \"coo\":\n1185 |             index_keys = [\"col\", \"row\"]\n1186 |         elif X.format in [\"csr\", \"csc\", \"bsr\"]:\n1187 |             index_keys = [\"indices\", \"indptr\"]\n1188 |         else:\n1189 |             return\n1190 |         for key in index_keys:\n1191 |             indices_datatype = getattr(X, key).dtype\n1192 |             if indices_datatype not in supported_indices:\n1193 |                 raise ValueError(\n1194 |                     \"Only sparse matrices with 32-bit integer indices are accepted.\"\n1195 |                     f\" Got {indices_datatype} indices. Please do report a minimal\"\n1196 |                     \" reproducer on scikit-learn issue tracker so that support for\"\n1197 |                     \" your use-case can be studied by maintainers. See:\"\n1198 |                     \" https://scikit-learn.org/dev/developers/minimal_reproducer.html\"\n1199 |                 )\n1200 | \n1201 | \n1202 | def check_X_y(\n1203 |     X,\n1204 |     y,\n1205 |     accept_sparse=False,\n1206 |     *,\n1207 |     accept_large_sparse=True,\n1208 |     dtype=\"numeric\",\n1209 |     order=None,\n1210 |     copy=False,\n1211 |     force_writeable=False,\n1212 |     force_all_finite=\"deprecated\",\n1213 |     ensure_all_finite=None,\n1214 |     ensure_2d=True,\n1215 |     allow_nd=False,\n1216 |     multi_output=False,\n1217 |     ensure_min_samples=1,\n1218 |     ensure_min_features=1,\n1219 |     y_numeric=False,\n1220 |     estimator=None,\n1221 | ):\n1222 |     \"\"\"Input validation for standard estimators.\n1223 | \n1224 |     Checks X and y for consistent length, enforces X to be 2D and y 1D. By\n1225 |     default, X is checked to be non-empty and containing only finite values.\n1226 |     Standard input checks are also applied to y, such as checking that y\n1227 |     does not have np.nan or np.inf targets. For multi-label y, set\n1228 |     multi_output=True to allow 2D and sparse y. If the dtype of X is\n1229 |     object, attempt converting to float, raising on failure.\n1230 | \n1231 |     Parameters\n1232 |     ----------\n1233 |     X : {ndarray, list, sparse matrix}\n1234 |         Input data.\n1235 | \n1236 |     y : {ndarray, list, sparse matrix}\n1237 |         Labels.\n1238 | \n1239 |     accept_sparse : str, bool or list of str, default=False\n1240 |         String[s] representing allowed sparse matrix formats, such as 'csc',\n1241 |         'csr', etc. If the input is sparse but not in the allowed format,\n1242 |         it will be converted to the first listed format. True allows the input\n1243 |         to be any format. False means that a sparse matrix input will\n1244 |         raise an error.\n1245 | \n1246 |     accept_large_sparse : bool, default=True\n1247 |         If a CSR, CSC, COO or BSR sparse matrix is supplied and accepted by\n1248 |         accept_sparse, accept_large_sparse will cause it to be accepted only\n1249 |         if its indices are stored with a 32-bit dtype.\n1250 | \n1251 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n1252 | \n1253 |     dtype : 'numeric', type, list of type or None, default='numeric'\n1254 |         Data type of result. If None, the dtype of the input is preserved.\n1255 |         If \"numeric\", dtype is preserved unless array.dtype is object.\n1256 |         If dtype is a list of types, conversion on the first type is only\n1257 |         performed if the dtype of the input is not in the list.\n1258 | \n1259 |     order : {'F', 'C'}, default=None\n1260 |         Whether an array will be forced to be fortran or c-style. If\n1261 |         `None`, then the input data's order is preserved when possible.\n1262 | \n1263 |     copy : bool, default=False\n1264 |         Whether a forced copy will be triggered. If copy=False, a copy might\n1265 |         be triggered by a conversion.\n1266 | \n1267 |     force_writeable : bool, default=False\n1268 |         Whether to force the output array to be writeable. If True, the returned array\n1269 |         is guaranteed to be writeable, which may require a copy. Otherwise the\n1270 |         writeability of the input array is preserved.\n1271 | \n1272 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n1273 | \n1274 |     force_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n1275 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array. This parameter\n1276 |         does not influence whether y can have np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA values.\n1277 |         The possibilities are:\n1278 | \n1279 |         - True: Force all values of X to be finite.\n1280 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in X.\n1281 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan or pd.NA values in X. Values cannot\n1282 |           be infinite.\n1283 | \n1284 |         .. versionadded:: 0.20\n1285 |            ``force_all_finite`` accepts the string ``'allow-nan'``.\n1286 | \n1287 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n1288 |            Accepts `pd.NA` and converts it into `np.nan`\n1289 | \n1290 |         .. deprecated:: 1.6\n1291 |            `force_all_finite` was renamed to `ensure_all_finite` and will be removed\n1292 |            in 1.8.\n1293 | \n1294 |     ensure_all_finite : bool or 'allow-nan', default=True\n1295 |         Whether to raise an error on np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in array. This parameter\n1296 |         does not influence whether y can have np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA values.\n1297 |         The possibilities are:\n1298 | \n1299 |         - True: Force all values of X to be finite.\n1300 |         - False: accepts np.inf, np.nan, pd.NA in X.\n1301 |         - 'allow-nan': accepts only np.nan or pd.NA values in X. Values cannot\n1302 |           be infinite.\n1303 | \n1304 |         .. versionadded:: 1.6\n1305 |            `force_all_finite` was renamed to `ensure_all_finite`.\n1306 | \n1307 |     ensure_2d : bool, default=True\n1308 |         Whether to raise a value error if X is not 2D.\n1309 | \n1310 |     allow_nd : bool, default=False\n1311 |         Whether to allow X.ndim > 2.\n1312 | \n1313 |     multi_output : bool, default=False\n1314 |         Whether to allow 2D y (array or sparse matrix). If false, y will be\n1315 |         validated as a vector. y cannot have np.nan or np.inf values if\n1316 |         multi_output=True.\n1317 | \n1318 |     ensure_min_samples : int, default=1\n1319 |         Make sure that X has a minimum number of samples in its first\n1320 |         axis (rows for a 2D array).\n1321 | \n1322 |     ensure_min_features : int, default=1\n1323 |         Make sure that the 2D array has some minimum number of features\n1324 |         (columns). The default value of 1 rejects empty datasets.\n1325 |         This check is only enforced when X has effectively 2 dimensions or\n1326 |         is originally 1D and ``ensure_2d`` is True. Setting to 0 disables\n1327 |         this check.\n1328 | \n1329 |     y_numeric : bool, default=False\n1330 |         Whether to ensure that y has a numeric type. If dtype of y is object,\n1331 |         it is converted to float64. Should only be used for regression\n1332 |         algorithms.\n1333 | \n1334 |     estimator : str or estimator instance, default=None\n1335 |         If passed, include the name of the estimator in warning messages.\n1336 | \n1337 |     Returns\n1338 |     -------\n1339 |     X_converted : object\n1340 |         The converted and validated X.\n1341 | \n1342 |     y_converted : object\n1343 |         The converted and validated y.\n1344 | \n1345 |     Examples\n1346 |     --------\n1347 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_X_y\n1348 |     >>> X = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]\n1349 |     >>> y = [1, 2, 3]\n1350 |     >>> X, y = check_X_y(X, y)\n1351 |     >>> X\n1352 |     array([[1, 2],\n1353 |           [3, 4],\n1354 |           [5, 6]])\n1355 |     >>> y\n1356 |     array([1, 2, 3])\n1357 |     \"\"\"\n1358 |     if y is None:\n1359 |         if estimator is None:\n1360 |             estimator_name = \"estimator\"\n1361 |         else:\n1362 |             estimator_name = _check_estimator_name(estimator)\n1363 |         raise ValueError(\n1364 |             f\"{estimator_name} requires y to be passed, but the target y is None\"\n1365 |         )\n1366 | \n1367 |     ensure_all_finite = _deprecate_force_all_finite(force_all_finite, ensure_all_finite)\n1368 | \n1369 |     X = check_array(\n1370 |         X,\n1371 |         accept_sparse=accept_sparse,\n1372 |         accept_large_sparse=accept_large_sparse,\n1373 |         dtype=dtype,\n1374 |         order=order,\n1375 |         copy=copy,\n1376 |         force_writeable=force_writeable,\n1377 |         ensure_all_finite=ensure_all_finite,\n1378 |         ensure_2d=ensure_2d,\n1379 |         allow_nd=allow_nd,\n1380 |         ensure_min_samples=ensure_min_samples,\n1381 |         ensure_min_features=ensure_min_features,\n1382 |         estimator=estimator,\n1383 |         input_name=\"X\",\n1384 |     )\n1385 | \n1386 |     y = _check_y(y, multi_output=multi_output, y_numeric=y_numeric, estimator=estimator)\n1387 | \n1388 |     check_consistent_length(X, y)\n1389 | \n1390 |     return X, y\n1391 | \n1392 | \n1393 | def _check_y(y, multi_output=False, y_numeric=False, estimator=None):\n1394 |     \"\"\"Isolated part of check_X_y dedicated to y validation\"\"\"\n1395 |     if multi_output:\n1396 |         y = check_array(\n1397 |             y,\n1398 |             accept_sparse=\"csr\",\n1399 |             ensure_all_finite=True,\n1400 |             ensure_2d=False,\n1401 |             dtype=None,\n1402 |             input_name=\"y\",\n1403 |             estimator=estimator,\n1404 |         )\n1405 |     else:\n1406 |         estimator_name = _check_estimator_name(estimator)\n1407 |         y = column_or_1d(y, warn=True)\n1408 |         _assert_all_finite(y, input_name=\"y\", estimator_name=estimator_name)\n1409 |         _ensure_no_complex_data(y)\n1410 |     if y_numeric and hasattr(y.dtype, \"kind\") and y.dtype.kind == \"O\":\n1411 |         y = y.astype(np.float64)\n1412 | \n1413 |     return y\n1414 | \n1415 | \n1416 | def column_or_1d(y, *, dtype=None, warn=False):\n1417 |     \"\"\"Ravel column or 1d numpy array, else raises an error.\n1418 | \n1419 |     Parameters\n1420 |     ----------\n1421 |     y : array-like\n1422 |        Input data.\n1423 | \n1424 |     dtype : data-type, default=None\n1425 |         Data type for `y`.\n1426 | \n1427 |         .. versionadded:: 1.2\n1428 | \n1429 |     warn : bool, default=False\n1430 |        To control display of warnings.\n1431 | \n1432 |     Returns\n1433 |     -------\n1434 |     y : ndarray\n1435 |        Output data.\n1436 | \n1437 |     Raises\n1438 |     ------\n1439 |     ValueError\n1440 |         If `y` is not a 1D array or a 2D array with a single row or column.\n1441 | \n1442 |     Examples\n1443 |     --------\n1444 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import column_or_1d\n1445 |     >>> column_or_1d([1, 1])\n1446 |     array([1, 1])\n1447 |     \"\"\"\n1448 |     xp, _ = get_namespace(y)\n1449 |     y = check_array(\n1450 |         y,\n1451 |         ensure_2d=False,\n1452 |         dtype=dtype,\n1453 |         input_name=\"y\",\n1454 |         ensure_all_finite=False,\n1455 |         ensure_min_samples=0,\n1456 |     )\n1457 | \n1458 |     shape = y.shape\n1459 |     if len(shape) == 1:\n1460 |         return _asarray_with_order(xp.reshape(y, (-1,)), order=\"C\", xp=xp)\n1461 |     if len(shape) == 2 and shape[1] == 1:\n1462 |         if warn:\n1463 |             warnings.warn(\n1464 |                 (\n1465 |                     \"A column-vector y was passed when a 1d array was\"\n1466 |                     \" expected. Please change the shape of y to \"\n1467 |                     \"(n_samples, ), for example using ravel().\"\n1468 |                 ),\n1469 |                 DataConversionWarning,\n1470 |                 stacklevel=2,\n1471 |             )\n1472 |         return _asarray_with_order(xp.reshape(y, (-1,)), order=\"C\", xp=xp)\n1473 | \n1474 |     raise ValueError(\n1475 |         \"y should be a 1d array, got an array of shape {} instead.\".format(shape)\n1476 |     )\n1477 | \n1478 | \n1479 | def check_random_state(seed):\n1480 |     \"\"\"Turn seed into a np.random.RandomState instance.\n1481 | \n1482 |     Parameters\n1483 |     ----------\n1484 |     seed : None, int or instance of RandomState\n1485 |         If seed is None, return the RandomState singleton used by np.random.\n1486 |         If seed is an int, return a new RandomState instance seeded with seed.\n1487 |         If seed is already a RandomState instance, return it.\n1488 |         Otherwise raise ValueError.\n1489 | \n1490 |     Returns\n1491 |     -------\n1492 |     :class:`numpy:numpy.random.RandomState`\n1493 |         The random state object based on `seed` parameter.\n1494 | \n1495 |     Examples\n1496 |     --------\n1497 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_random_state\n1498 |     >>> check_random_state(42)\n1499 |     RandomState(MT19937) at 0x...\n1500 |     \"\"\"\n1501 |     if seed is None or seed is np.random:\n1502 |         return np.random.mtrand._rand\n1503 |     if isinstance(seed, numbers.Integral):\n1504 |         return np.random.RandomState(seed)\n1505 |     if isinstance(seed, np.random.RandomState):\n1506 |         return seed\n1507 |     raise ValueError(\n1508 |         \"%r cannot be used to seed a numpy.random.RandomState instance\" % seed\n1509 |     )\n1510 | \n1511 | \n1512 | def has_fit_parameter(estimator, parameter):\n1513 |     \"\"\"Check whether the estimator's fit method supports the given parameter.\n1514 | \n1515 |     Parameters\n1516 |     ----------\n1517 |     estimator : object\n1518 |         An estimator to inspect.\n1519 | \n1520 |     parameter : str\n1521 |         The searched parameter.\n1522 | \n1523 |     Returns\n1524 |     -------\n1525 |     is_parameter : bool\n1526 |         Whether the parameter was found to be a named parameter of the\n1527 |         estimator's fit method.\n1528 | \n1529 |     Examples\n1530 |     --------\n1531 |     >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n1532 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import has_fit_parameter\n1533 |     >>> has_fit_parameter(SVC(), \"sample_weight\")\n1534 |     True\n1535 |     \"\"\"\n1536 |     return (\n1537 |         # This is used during test collection in common tests. The\n1538 |         # hasattr(estimator, \"fit\") makes it so that we don't fail for an estimator\n1539 |         # that does not have a `fit` method during collection of checks. The right\n1540 |         # checks will fail later.\n1541 |         hasattr(estimator, \"fit\")\n1542 |         and parameter in signature(estimator.fit).parameters\n1543 |     )\n1544 | \n1545 | \n1546 | def check_symmetric(array, *, tol=1e-10, raise_warning=True, raise_exception=False):\n1547 |     \"\"\"Make sure that array is 2D, square and symmetric.\n1548 | \n1549 |     If the array is not symmetric, then a symmetrized version is returned.\n1550 |     Optionally, a warning or exception is raised if the matrix is not\n1551 |     symmetric.\n1552 | \n1553 |     Parameters\n1554 |     ----------\n1555 |     array : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n1556 |         Input object to check / convert. Must be two-dimensional and square,\n1557 |         otherwise a ValueError will be raised.\n1558 | \n1559 |     tol : float, default=1e-10\n1560 |         Absolute tolerance for equivalence of arrays. Default = 1E-10.\n1561 | \n1562 |     raise_warning : bool, default=True\n1563 |         If True then raise a warning if conversion is required.\n1564 | \n1565 |     raise_exception : bool, default=False\n1566 |         If True then raise an exception if array is not symmetric.\n1567 | \n1568 |     Returns\n1569 |     -------\n1570 |     array_sym : {ndarray, sparse matrix}\n1571 |         Symmetrized version of the input array, i.e. the average of array\n1572 |         and array.transpose(). If sparse, then duplicate entries are first\n1573 |         summed and zeros are eliminated.\n1574 | \n1575 |     Examples\n1576 |     --------\n1577 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1578 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_symmetric\n1579 |     >>> symmetric_array = np.array([[0, 1, 2], [1, 0, 1], [2, 1, 0]])\n1580 |     >>> check_symmetric(symmetric_array)\n1581 |     array([[0, 1, 2],\n1582 |            [1, 0, 1],\n1583 |            [2, 1, 0]])\n1584 |     >>> from scipy.sparse import csr_matrix\n1585 |     >>> sparse_symmetric_array = csr_matrix(symmetric_array)\n1586 |     >>> check_symmetric(sparse_symmetric_array)\n1587 |     <Compressed Sparse Row sparse matrix of dtype 'int64'\n1588 |         with 6 stored elements and shape (3, 3)>\n1589 |     \"\"\"\n1590 |     if (array.ndim != 2) or (array.shape[0] != array.shape[1]):\n1591 |         raise ValueError(\n1592 |             \"array must be 2-dimensional and square. shape = {0}\".format(array.shape)\n1593 |         )\n1594 | \n1595 |     if sp.issparse(array):\n1596 |         diff = array - array.T\n1597 |         # only csr, csc, and coo have `data` attribute\n1598 |         if diff.format not in [\"csr\", \"csc\", \"coo\"]:\n1599 |             diff = diff.tocsr()\n1600 |         symmetric = np.all(abs(diff.data) < tol)\n1601 |     else:\n1602 |         symmetric = np.allclose(array, array.T, atol=tol)\n1603 | \n1604 |     if not symmetric:\n1605 |         if raise_exception:\n1606 |             raise ValueError(\"Array must be symmetric\")\n1607 |         if raise_warning:\n1608 |             warnings.warn(\n1609 |                 (\n1610 |                     \"Array is not symmetric, and will be converted \"\n1611 |                     \"to symmetric by average with its transpose.\"\n1612 |                 ),\n1613 |                 stacklevel=2,\n1614 |             )\n1615 |         if sp.issparse(array):\n1616 |             conversion = \"to\" + array.format\n1617 |             array = getattr(0.5 * (array + array.T), conversion)()\n1618 |         else:\n1619 |             array = 0.5 * (array + array.T)\n1620 | \n1621 |     return array\n1622 | \n1623 | \n1624 | def _is_fitted(estimator, attributes=None, all_or_any=all):\n1625 |     \"\"\"Determine if an estimator is fitted\n1626 | \n1627 |     Parameters\n1628 |     ----------\n1629 |     estimator : estimator instance\n1630 |         Estimator instance for which the check is performed.\n1631 | \n1632 |     attributes : str, list or tuple of str, default=None\n1633 |         Attribute name(s) given as string or a list/tuple of strings\n1634 |         Eg.: ``[\"coef_\", \"estimator_\", ...], \"coef_\"``\n1635 | \n1636 |         If `None`, `estimator` is considered fitted if there exist an\n1637 |         attribute that ends with a underscore and does not start with double\n1638 |         underscore.\n1639 | \n1640 |     all_or_any : callable, {all, any}, default=all\n1641 |         Specify whether all or any of the given attributes must exist.\n1642 | \n1643 |     Returns\n1644 |     -------\n1645 |     fitted : bool\n1646 |         Whether the estimator is fitted.\n1647 |     \"\"\"\n1648 |     if attributes is not None:\n1649 |         if not isinstance(attributes, (list, tuple)):\n1650 |             attributes = [attributes]\n1651 |         return all_or_any([hasattr(estimator, attr) for attr in attributes])\n1652 | \n1653 |     if hasattr(estimator, \"__sklearn_is_fitted__\"):\n1654 |         return estimator.__sklearn_is_fitted__()\n1655 | \n1656 |     fitted_attrs = [\n1657 |         v for v in vars(estimator) if v.endswith(\"_\") and not v.startswith(\"__\")\n1658 |     ]\n1659 |     return len(fitted_attrs) > 0\n1660 | \n1661 | \n1662 | def check_is_fitted(estimator, attributes=None, *, msg=None, all_or_any=all):\n1663 |     \"\"\"Perform is_fitted validation for estimator.\n1664 | \n1665 |     Checks if the estimator is fitted by verifying the presence of\n1666 |     fitted attributes (ending with a trailing underscore) and otherwise\n1667 |     raises a :class:`~sklearn.exceptions.NotFittedError` with the given message.\n1668 | \n1669 |     If an estimator does not set any attributes with a trailing underscore, it\n1670 |     can define a ``__sklearn_is_fitted__`` method returning a boolean to\n1671 |     specify if the estimator is fitted or not. See\n1672 |     :ref:`sphx_glr_auto_examples_developing_estimators_sklearn_is_fitted.py`\n1673 |     for an example on how to use the API.\n1674 | \n1675 |     If no `attributes` are passed, this fuction will pass if an estimator is stateless.\n1676 |     An estimator can indicate it's stateless by setting the `requires_fit` tag. See\n1677 |     :ref:`estimator_tags` for more information. Note that the `requires_fit` tag\n1678 |     is ignored if `attributes` are passed.\n1679 | \n1680 |     Parameters\n1681 |     ----------\n1682 |     estimator : estimator instance\n1683 |         Estimator instance for which the check is performed.\n1684 | \n1685 |     attributes : str, list or tuple of str, default=None\n1686 |         Attribute name(s) given as string or a list/tuple of strings\n1687 |         Eg.: ``[\"coef_\", \"estimator_\", ...], \"coef_\"``\n1688 | \n1689 |         If `None`, `estimator` is considered fitted if there exist an\n1690 |         attribute that ends with a underscore and does not start with double\n1691 |         underscore.\n1692 | \n1693 |     msg : str, default=None\n1694 |         The default error message is, \"This %(name)s instance is not fitted\n1695 |         yet. Call 'fit' with appropriate arguments before using this\n1696 |         estimator.\"\n1697 | \n1698 |         For custom messages if \"%(name)s\" is present in the message string,\n1699 |         it is substituted for the estimator name.\n1700 | \n1701 |         Eg. : \"Estimator, %(name)s, must be fitted before sparsifying\".\n1702 | \n1703 |     all_or_any : callable, {all, any}, default=all\n1704 |         Specify whether all or any of the given attributes must exist.\n1705 | \n1706 |     Raises\n1707 |     ------\n1708 |     TypeError\n1709 |         If the estimator is a class or not an estimator instance\n1710 | \n1711 |     NotFittedError\n1712 |         If the attributes are not found.\n1713 | \n1714 |     Examples\n1715 |     --------\n1716 |     >>> from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n1717 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_is_fitted\n1718 |     >>> from sklearn.exceptions import NotFittedError\n1719 |     >>> lr = LogisticRegression()\n1720 |     >>> try:\n1721 |     ...     check_is_fitted(lr)\n1722 |     ... except NotFittedError as exc:\n1723 |     ...     print(f\"Model is not fitted yet.\")\n1724 |     Model is not fitted yet.\n1725 |     >>> lr.fit([[1, 2], [1, 3]], [1, 0])\n1726 |     LogisticRegression()\n1727 |     >>> check_is_fitted(lr)\n1728 |     \"\"\"\n1729 |     if isclass(estimator):\n1730 |         raise TypeError(\"{} is a class, not an instance.\".format(estimator))\n1731 |     if msg is None:\n1732 |         msg = (\n1733 |             \"This %(name)s instance is not fitted yet. Call 'fit' with \"\n1734 |             \"appropriate arguments before using this estimator.\"\n1735 |         )\n1736 | \n1737 |     if not hasattr(estimator, \"fit\"):\n1738 |         raise TypeError(\"%s is not an estimator instance.\" % (estimator))\n1739 | \n1740 |     tags = get_tags(estimator)\n1741 | \n1742 |     if not tags.requires_fit and attributes is None:\n1743 |         return\n1744 | \n1745 |     if not _is_fitted(estimator, attributes, all_or_any):\n1746 |         raise NotFittedError(msg % {\"name\": type(estimator).__name__})\n1747 | \n1748 | \n1749 | def _estimator_has(attr, *, delegates=(\"estimator_\", \"estimator\")):\n1750 |     \"\"\"Check if we can delegate a method to the underlying estimator.\n1751 | \n1752 |     We check the `delegates` in the order they are passed. By default, we first check\n1753 |     the fitted estimator if available, otherwise we check the unfitted estimator.\n1754 | \n1755 |     Parameters\n1756 |     ----------\n1757 |     attr : str\n1758 |         Name of the attribute the delegate might or might not have.\n1759 | \n1760 |     delegates: tuple of str, default=(\"estimator_\", \"estimator\")\n1761 |         A tuple of sub-estimator(s) to check if we can delegate the `attr` method.\n1762 | \n1763 |     Returns\n1764 |     -------\n1765 |     check : function\n1766 |         Function to check if the delegate has the attribute.\n1767 | \n1768 |     Raises\n1769 |     ------\n1770 |     ValueError\n1771 |         Raised when none of the delegates are present in the object.\n1772 |     \"\"\"\n1773 | \n1774 |     def check(self):\n1775 |         for delegate in delegates:\n1776 |             # In meta estimators with multiple sub estimators,\n1777 |             # only the attribute of the first sub estimator is checked,\n1778 |             # assuming uniformity across all sub estimators.\n1779 |             if hasattr(self, delegate):\n1780 |                 delegator = getattr(self, delegate)\n1781 |                 if isinstance(delegator, Sequence):\n1782 |                     return getattr(delegator[0], attr)\n1783 |                 else:\n1784 |                     return getattr(delegator, attr)\n1785 | \n1786 |         raise ValueError(f\"None of the delegates {delegates} are present in the class.\")\n1787 | \n1788 |     return check\n1789 | \n1790 | \n1791 | def check_non_negative(X, whom):\n1792 |     \"\"\"\n1793 |     Check if there is any negative value in an array.\n1794 | \n1795 |     Parameters\n1796 |     ----------\n1797 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix}\n1798 |         Input data.\n1799 | \n1800 |     whom : str\n1801 |         Who passed X to this function.\n1802 |     \"\"\"\n1803 |     xp, _ = get_namespace(X)\n1804 |     # avoid X.min() on sparse matrix since it also sorts the indices\n1805 |     if sp.issparse(X):\n1806 |         if X.format in [\"lil\", \"dok\"]:\n1807 |             X = X.tocsr()\n1808 |         if X.data.size == 0:\n1809 |             X_min = 0\n1810 |         else:\n1811 |             X_min = X.data.min()\n1812 |     else:\n1813 |         X_min = xp.min(X)\n1814 | \n1815 |     if X_min < 0:\n1816 |         raise ValueError(f\"Negative values in data passed to {whom}.\")\n1817 | \n1818 | \n1819 | def check_scalar(\n1820 |     x,\n1821 |     name,\n1822 |     target_type,\n1823 |     *,\n1824 |     min_val=None,\n1825 |     max_val=None,\n1826 |     include_boundaries=\"both\",\n1827 | ):\n1828 |     \"\"\"Validate scalar parameters type and value.\n1829 | \n1830 |     Parameters\n1831 |     ----------\n1832 |     x : object\n1833 |         The scalar parameter to validate.\n1834 | \n1835 |     name : str\n1836 |         The name of the parameter to be printed in error messages.\n1837 | \n1838 |     target_type : type or tuple\n1839 |         Acceptable data types for the parameter.\n1840 | \n1841 |     min_val : float or int, default=None\n1842 |         The minimum valid value the parameter can take. If None (default) it\n1843 |         is implied that the parameter does not have a lower bound.\n1844 | \n1845 |     max_val : float or int, default=None\n1846 |         The maximum valid value the parameter can take. If None (default) it\n1847 |         is implied that the parameter does not have an upper bound.\n1848 | \n1849 |     include_boundaries : {\"left\", \"right\", \"both\", \"neither\"}, default=\"both\"\n1850 |         Whether the interval defined by `min_val` and `max_val` should include\n1851 |         the boundaries. Possible choices are:\n1852 | \n1853 |         - `\"left\"`: only `min_val` is included in the valid interval.\n1854 |           It is equivalent to the interval `[ min_val, max_val )`.\n1855 |         - `\"right\"`: only `max_val` is included in the valid interval.\n1856 |           It is equivalent to the interval `( min_val, max_val ]`.\n1857 |         - `\"both\"`: `min_val` and `max_val` are included in the valid interval.\n1858 |           It is equivalent to the interval `[ min_val, max_val ]`.\n1859 |         - `\"neither\"`: neither `min_val` nor `max_val` are included in the\n1860 |           valid interval. It is equivalent to the interval `( min_val, max_val )`.\n1861 | \n1862 |     Returns\n1863 |     -------\n1864 |     x : numbers.Number\n1865 |         The validated number.\n1866 | \n1867 |     Raises\n1868 |     ------\n1869 |     TypeError\n1870 |         If the parameter's type does not match the desired type.\n1871 | \n1872 |     ValueError\n1873 |         If the parameter's value violates the given bounds.\n1874 |         If `min_val`, `max_val` and `include_boundaries` are inconsistent.\n1875 | \n1876 |     Examples\n1877 |     --------\n1878 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import check_scalar\n1879 |     >>> check_scalar(10, \"x\", int, min_val=1, max_val=20)\n1880 |     10\n1881 |     \"\"\"\n1882 | \n1883 |     def type_name(t):\n1884 |         \"\"\"Convert type into humman readable string.\"\"\"\n1885 |         module = t.__module__\n1886 |         qualname = t.__qualname__\n1887 |         if module == \"builtins\":\n1888 |             return qualname\n1889 |         elif t == numbers.Real:\n1890 |             return \"float\"\n1891 |         elif t == numbers.Integral:\n1892 |             return \"int\"\n1893 |         return f\"{module}.{qualname}\"\n1894 | \n1895 |     if not isinstance(x, target_type):\n1896 |         if isinstance(target_type, tuple):\n1897 |             types_str = \", \".join(type_name(t) for t in target_type)\n1898 |             target_type_str = f\"{{{types_str}}}\"\n1899 |         else:\n1900 |             target_type_str = type_name(target_type)\n1901 | \n1902 |         raise TypeError(\n1903 |             f\"{name} must be an instance of {target_type_str}, not\"\n1904 |             f\" {type(x).__qualname__}.\"\n1905 |         )\n1906 | \n1907 |     expected_include_boundaries = (\"left\", \"right\", \"both\", \"neither\")\n1908 |     if include_boundaries not in expected_include_boundaries:\n1909 |         raise ValueError(\n1910 |             f\"Unknown value for `include_boundaries`: {repr(include_boundaries)}. \"\n1911 |             f\"Possible values are: {expected_include_boundaries}.\"\n1912 |         )\n1913 | \n1914 |     if max_val is None and include_boundaries == \"right\":\n1915 |         raise ValueError(\n1916 |             \"`include_boundaries`='right' without specifying explicitly `max_val` \"\n1917 |             \"is inconsistent.\"\n1918 |         )\n1919 | \n1920 |     if min_val is None and include_boundaries == \"left\":\n1921 |         raise ValueError(\n1922 |             \"`include_boundaries`='left' without specifying explicitly `min_val` \"\n1923 |             \"is inconsistent.\"\n1924 |         )\n1925 | \n1926 |     comparison_operator = (\n1927 |         operator.lt if include_boundaries in (\"left\", \"both\") else operator.le\n1928 |     )\n1929 |     if min_val is not None and comparison_operator(x, min_val):\n1930 |         raise ValueError(\n1931 |             f\"{name} == {x}, must be\"\n1932 |             f\" {'>=' if include_boundaries in ('left', 'both') else '>'} {min_val}.\"\n1933 |         )\n1934 | \n1935 |     comparison_operator = (\n1936 |         operator.gt if include_boundaries in (\"right\", \"both\") else operator.ge\n1937 |     )\n1938 |     if max_val is not None and comparison_operator(x, max_val):\n1939 |         raise ValueError(\n1940 |             f\"{name} == {x}, must be\"\n1941 |             f\" {'<=' if include_boundaries in ('right', 'both') else '<'} {max_val}.\"\n1942 |         )\n1943 | \n1944 |     return x\n1945 | \n1946 | \n1947 | def _check_psd_eigenvalues(lambdas, enable_warnings=False):\n1948 |     \"\"\"Check the eigenvalues of a positive semidefinite (PSD) matrix.\n1949 | \n1950 |     Checks the provided array of PSD matrix eigenvalues for numerical or\n1951 |     conditioning issues and returns a fixed validated version. This method\n1952 |     should typically be used if the PSD matrix is user-provided (e.g. a\n1953 |     Gram matrix) or computed using a user-provided dissimilarity metric\n1954 |     (e.g. kernel function), or if the decomposition process uses approximation\n1955 |     methods (randomized SVD, etc.).\n1956 | \n1957 |     It checks for three things:\n1958 | \n1959 |     - that there are no significant imaginary parts in eigenvalues (more than\n1960 |       1e-5 times the maximum real part). If this check fails, it raises a\n1961 |       ``ValueError``. Otherwise all non-significant imaginary parts that may\n1962 |       remain are set to zero. This operation is traced with a\n1963 |       ``PositiveSpectrumWarning`` when ``enable_warnings=True``.\n1964 | \n1965 |     - that eigenvalues are not all negative. If this check fails, it raises a\n1966 |       ``ValueError``\n1967 | \n1968 |     - that there are no significant negative eigenvalues with absolute value\n1969 |       more than 1e-10 (1e-6) and more than 1e-5 (5e-3) times the largest\n1970 |       positive eigenvalue in double (simple) precision. If this check fails,\n1971 |       it raises a ``ValueError``. Otherwise all negative eigenvalues that may\n1972 |       remain are set to zero. This operation is traced with a\n1973 |       ``PositiveSpectrumWarning`` when ``enable_warnings=True``.\n1974 | \n1975 |     Finally, all the positive eigenvalues that are too small (with a value\n1976 |     smaller than the maximum eigenvalue multiplied by 1e-12 (2e-7)) are set to\n1977 |     zero. This operation is traced with a ``PositiveSpectrumWarning`` when\n1978 |     ``enable_warnings=True``.\n1979 | \n1980 |     Parameters\n1981 |     ----------\n1982 |     lambdas : array-like of shape (n_eigenvalues,)\n1983 |         Array of eigenvalues to check / fix.\n1984 | \n1985 |     enable_warnings : bool, default=False\n1986 |         When this is set to ``True``, a ``PositiveSpectrumWarning`` will be\n1987 |         raised when there are imaginary parts, negative eigenvalues, or\n1988 |         extremely small non-zero eigenvalues. Otherwise no warning will be\n1989 |         raised. In both cases, imaginary parts, negative eigenvalues, and\n1990 |         extremely small non-zero eigenvalues will be set to zero.\n1991 | \n1992 |     Returns\n1993 |     -------\n1994 |     lambdas_fixed : ndarray of shape (n_eigenvalues,)\n1995 |         A fixed validated copy of the array of eigenvalues.\n1996 | \n1997 |     Examples\n1998 |     --------\n1999 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import _check_psd_eigenvalues\n2000 |     >>> _check_psd_eigenvalues([1, 2])      # nominal case\n2001 |     array([1, 2])\n2002 |     >>> _check_psd_eigenvalues([5, 5j])     # significant imag part\n2003 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n2004 |         ...\n2005 |     ValueError: There are significant imaginary parts in eigenvalues (1\n2006 |         of the maximum real part). Either the matrix is not PSD, or there was\n2007 |         an issue while computing the eigendecomposition of the matrix.\n2008 |     >>> _check_psd_eigenvalues([5, 5e-5j])  # insignificant imag part\n2009 |     array([5., 0.])\n2010 |     >>> _check_psd_eigenvalues([-5, -1])    # all negative\n2011 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n2012 |         ...\n2013 |     ValueError: All eigenvalues are negative (maximum is -1). Either the\n2014 |         matrix is not PSD, or there was an issue while computing the\n2015 |         eigendecomposition of the matrix.\n2016 |     >>> _check_psd_eigenvalues([5, -1])     # significant negative\n2017 |     Traceback (most recent call last):\n2018 |         ...\n2019 |     ValueError: There are significant negative eigenvalues (0.2 of the\n2020 |         maximum positive). Either the matrix is not PSD, or there was an issue\n2021 |         while computing the eigendecomposition of the matrix.\n2022 |     >>> _check_psd_eigenvalues([5, -5e-5])  # insignificant negative\n2023 |     array([5., 0.])\n2024 |     >>> _check_psd_eigenvalues([5, 4e-12])  # bad conditioning (too small)\n2025 |     array([5., 0.])\n2026 | \n2027 |     \"\"\"\n2028 | \n2029 |     lambdas = np.array(lambdas)\n2030 |     is_double_precision = lambdas.dtype == np.float64\n2031 | \n2032 |     # note: the minimum value available is\n2033 |     #  - single-precision: np.finfo('float32').eps = 1.2e-07\n2034 |     #  - double-precision: np.finfo('float64').eps = 2.2e-16\n2035 | \n2036 |     # the various thresholds used for validation\n2037 |     # we may wish to change the value according to precision.\n2038 |     significant_imag_ratio = 1e-5\n2039 |     significant_neg_ratio = 1e-5 if is_double_precision else 5e-3\n2040 |     significant_neg_value = 1e-10 if is_double_precision else 1e-6\n2041 |     small_pos_ratio = 1e-12 if is_double_precision else 2e-7\n2042 | \n2043 |     # Check that there are no significant imaginary parts\n2044 |     if not np.isreal(lambdas).all():\n2045 |         max_imag_abs = np.abs(np.imag(lambdas)).max()\n2046 |         max_real_abs = np.abs(np.real(lambdas)).max()\n2047 |         if max_imag_abs > significant_imag_ratio * max_real_abs:\n2048 |             raise ValueError(\n2049 |                 \"There are significant imaginary parts in eigenvalues (%g \"\n2050 |                 \"of the maximum real part). Either the matrix is not PSD, or \"\n2051 |                 \"there was an issue while computing the eigendecomposition \"\n2052 |                 \"of the matrix.\" % (max_imag_abs / max_real_abs)\n2053 |             )\n2054 | \n2055 |         # warn about imaginary parts being removed\n2056 |         if enable_warnings:\n2057 |             warnings.warn(\n2058 |                 \"There are imaginary parts in eigenvalues (%g \"\n2059 |                 \"of the maximum real part). Either the matrix is not\"\n2060 |                 \" PSD, or there was an issue while computing the \"\n2061 |                 \"eigendecomposition of the matrix. Only the real \"\n2062 |                 \"parts will be kept.\" % (max_imag_abs / max_real_abs),\n2063 |                 PositiveSpectrumWarning,\n2064 |             )\n2065 | \n2066 |     # Remove all imaginary parts (even if zero)\n2067 |     lambdas = np.real(lambdas)\n2068 | \n2069 |     # Check that there are no significant negative eigenvalues\n2070 |     max_eig = lambdas.max()\n2071 |     if max_eig < 0:\n2072 |         raise ValueError(\n2073 |             \"All eigenvalues are negative (maximum is %g). \"\n2074 |             \"Either the matrix is not PSD, or there was an \"\n2075 |             \"issue while computing the eigendecomposition of \"\n2076 |             \"the matrix.\" % max_eig\n2077 |         )\n2078 | \n2079 |     else:\n2080 |         min_eig = lambdas.min()\n2081 |         if (\n2082 |             min_eig < -significant_neg_ratio * max_eig\n2083 |             and min_eig < -significant_neg_value\n2084 |         ):\n2085 |             raise ValueError(\n2086 |                 \"There are significant negative eigenvalues (%g\"\n2087 |                 \" of the maximum positive). Either the matrix is \"\n2088 |                 \"not PSD, or there was an issue while computing \"\n2089 |                 \"the eigendecomposition of the matrix.\" % (-min_eig / max_eig)\n2090 |             )\n2091 |         elif min_eig < 0:\n2092 |             # Remove all negative values and warn about it\n2093 |             if enable_warnings:\n2094 |                 warnings.warn(\n2095 |                     \"There are negative eigenvalues (%g of the \"\n2096 |                     \"maximum positive). Either the matrix is not \"\n2097 |                     \"PSD, or there was an issue while computing the\"\n2098 |                     \" eigendecomposition of the matrix. Negative \"\n2099 |                     \"eigenvalues will be replaced with 0.\" % (-min_eig / max_eig),\n2100 |                     PositiveSpectrumWarning,\n2101 |                 )\n2102 |             lambdas[lambdas < 0] = 0\n2103 | \n2104 |     # Check for conditioning (small positive non-zeros)\n2105 |     too_small_lambdas = (0 < lambdas) & (lambdas < small_pos_ratio * max_eig)\n2106 |     if too_small_lambdas.any():\n2107 |         if enable_warnings:\n2108 |             warnings.warn(\n2109 |                 \"Badly conditioned PSD matrix spectrum: the largest \"\n2110 |                 \"eigenvalue is more than %g times the smallest. \"\n2111 |                 \"Small eigenvalues will be replaced with 0.\"\n2112 |                 \"\" % (1 / small_pos_ratio),\n2113 |                 PositiveSpectrumWarning,\n2114 |             )\n2115 |         lambdas[too_small_lambdas] = 0\n2116 | \n2117 |     return lambdas\n2118 | \n2119 | \n2120 | def _check_sample_weight(\n2121 |     sample_weight, X, dtype=None, copy=False, ensure_non_negative=False\n2122 | ):\n2123 |     \"\"\"Validate sample weights.\n2124 | \n2125 |     Note that passing sample_weight=None will output an array of ones.\n2126 |     Therefore, in some cases, you may want to protect the call with:\n2127 |     if sample_weight is not None:\n2128 |         sample_weight = _check_sample_weight(...)\n2129 | \n2130 |     Parameters\n2131 |     ----------\n2132 |     sample_weight : {ndarray, Number or None}, shape (n_samples,)\n2133 |         Input sample weights.\n2134 | \n2135 |     X : {ndarray, list, sparse matrix}\n2136 |         Input data.\n2137 | \n2138 |     ensure_non_negative : bool, default=False,\n2139 |         Whether or not the weights are expected to be non-negative.\n2140 | \n2141 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n2142 | \n2143 |     dtype : dtype, default=None\n2144 |         dtype of the validated `sample_weight`.\n2145 |         If None, and the input `sample_weight` is an array, the dtype of the\n2146 |         input is preserved; otherwise an array with the default numpy dtype\n2147 |         is be allocated.  If `dtype` is not one of `float32`, `float64`,\n2148 |         `None`, the output will be of dtype `float64`.\n2149 | \n2150 |     copy : bool, default=False\n2151 |         If True, a copy of sample_weight will be created.\n2152 | \n2153 |     Returns\n2154 |     -------\n2155 |     sample_weight : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n2156 |         Validated sample weight. It is guaranteed to be \"C\" contiguous.\n2157 |     \"\"\"\n2158 |     n_samples = _num_samples(X)\n2159 | \n2160 |     if dtype is not None and dtype not in [np.float32, np.float64]:\n2161 |         dtype = np.float64\n2162 | \n2163 |     if sample_weight is None:\n2164 |         sample_weight = np.ones(n_samples, dtype=dtype)\n2165 |     elif isinstance(sample_weight, numbers.Number):\n2166 |         sample_weight = np.full(n_samples, sample_weight, dtype=dtype)\n2167 |     else:\n2168 |         if dtype is None:\n2169 |             dtype = [np.float64, np.float32]\n2170 |         sample_weight = check_array(\n2171 |             sample_weight,\n2172 |             accept_sparse=False,\n2173 |             ensure_2d=False,\n2174 |             dtype=dtype,\n2175 |             order=\"C\",\n2176 |             copy=copy,\n2177 |             input_name=\"sample_weight\",\n2178 |         )\n2179 |         if sample_weight.ndim != 1:\n2180 |             raise ValueError(\"Sample weights must be 1D array or scalar\")\n2181 | \n2182 |         if sample_weight.shape != (n_samples,):\n2183 |             raise ValueError(\n2184 |                 \"sample_weight.shape == {}, expected {}!\".format(\n2185 |                     sample_weight.shape, (n_samples,)\n2186 |                 )\n2187 |             )\n2188 | \n2189 |     if ensure_non_negative:\n2190 |         check_non_negative(sample_weight, \"`sample_weight`\")\n2191 | \n2192 |     return sample_weight\n2193 | \n2194 | \n2195 | def _allclose_dense_sparse(x, y, rtol=1e-7, atol=1e-9):\n2196 |     \"\"\"Check allclose for sparse and dense data.\n2197 | \n2198 |     Both x and y need to be either sparse or dense, they\n2199 |     can't be mixed.\n2200 | \n2201 |     Parameters\n2202 |     ----------\n2203 |     x : {array-like, sparse matrix}\n2204 |         First array to compare.\n2205 | \n2206 |     y : {array-like, sparse matrix}\n2207 |         Second array to compare.\n2208 | \n2209 |     rtol : float, default=1e-7\n2210 |         Relative tolerance; see numpy.allclose.\n2211 | \n2212 |     atol : float, default=1e-9\n2213 |         absolute tolerance; see numpy.allclose. Note that the default here is\n2214 |         more tolerant than the default for numpy.testing.assert_allclose, where\n2215 |         atol=0.\n2216 |     \"\"\"\n2217 |     if sp.issparse(x) and sp.issparse(y):\n2218 |         x = x.tocsr()\n2219 |         y = y.tocsr()\n2220 |         x.sum_duplicates()\n2221 |         y.sum_duplicates()\n2222 |         return (\n2223 |             np.array_equal(x.indices, y.indices)\n2224 |             and np.array_equal(x.indptr, y.indptr)\n2225 |             and np.allclose(x.data, y.data, rtol=rtol, atol=atol)\n2226 |         )\n2227 |     elif not sp.issparse(x) and not sp.issparse(y):\n2228 |         return np.allclose(x, y, rtol=rtol, atol=atol)\n2229 |     raise ValueError(\n2230 |         \"Can only compare two sparse matrices, not a sparse matrix and an array\"\n2231 |     )\n2232 | \n2233 | \n2234 | def _check_response_method(estimator, response_method):\n2235 |     \"\"\"Check if `response_method` is available in estimator and return it.\n2236 | \n2237 |     .. versionadded:: 1.3\n2238 | \n2239 |     Parameters\n2240 |     ----------\n2241 |     estimator : estimator instance\n2242 |         Classifier or regressor to check.\n2243 | \n2244 |     response_method : {\"predict_proba\", \"predict_log_proba\", \"decision_function\",\n2245 |             \"predict\"} or list of such str\n2246 |         Specifies the response method to use get prediction from an estimator\n2247 |         (i.e. :term:`predict_proba`, :term:`predict_log_proba`,\n2248 |         :term:`decision_function` or :term:`predict`). Possible choices are:\n2249 |         - if `str`, it corresponds to the name to the method to return;\n2250 |         - if a list of `str`, it provides the method names in order of\n2251 |           preference. The method returned corresponds to the first method in\n2252 |           the list and which is implemented by `estimator`.\n2253 | \n2254 |     Returns\n2255 |     -------\n2256 |     prediction_method : callable\n2257 |         Prediction method of estimator.\n2258 | \n2259 |     Raises\n2260 |     ------\n2261 |     AttributeError\n2262 |         If `response_method` is not available in `estimator`.\n2263 |     \"\"\"\n2264 |     if isinstance(response_method, str):\n2265 |         list_methods = [response_method]\n2266 |     else:\n2267 |         list_methods = response_method\n2268 | \n2269 |     prediction_method = [getattr(estimator, method, None) for method in list_methods]\n2270 |     prediction_method = reduce(lambda x, y: x or y, prediction_method)\n2271 |     if prediction_method is None:\n2272 |         raise AttributeError(\n2273 |             f\"{estimator.__class__.__name__} has none of the following attributes: \"\n2274 |             f\"{', '.join(list_methods)}.\"\n2275 |         )\n2276 | \n2277 |     return prediction_method\n2278 | \n2279 | \n2280 | def _check_method_params(X, params, indices=None):\n2281 |     \"\"\"Check and validate the parameters passed to a specific\n2282 |     method like `fit`.\n2283 | \n2284 |     Parameters\n2285 |     ----------\n2286 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n2287 |         Data array.\n2288 | \n2289 |     params : dict\n2290 |         Dictionary containing the parameters passed to the method.\n2291 | \n2292 |     indices : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n2293 |         Indices to be selected if the parameter has the same size as `X`.\n2294 | \n2295 |     Returns\n2296 |     -------\n2297 |     method_params_validated : dict\n2298 |         Validated parameters. We ensure that the values support indexing.\n2299 |     \"\"\"\n2300 |     from . import _safe_indexing\n2301 | \n2302 |     method_params_validated = {}\n2303 |     for param_key, param_value in params.items():\n2304 |         if (\n2305 |             not _is_arraylike(param_value)\n2306 |             and not sp.issparse(param_value)\n2307 |             or _num_samples(param_value) != _num_samples(X)\n2308 |         ):\n2309 |             # Non-indexable pass-through (for now for backward-compatibility).\n2310 |             # https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/issues/15805\n2311 |             method_params_validated[param_key] = param_value\n2312 |         else:\n2313 |             # Any other method_params should support indexing\n2314 |             # (e.g. for cross-validation).\n2315 |             method_params_validated[param_key] = _make_indexable(param_value)\n2316 |             method_params_validated[param_key] = _safe_indexing(\n2317 |                 method_params_validated[param_key], indices\n2318 |             )\n2319 | \n2320 |     return method_params_validated\n2321 | \n2322 | \n2323 | def _is_pandas_df_or_series(X):\n2324 |     \"\"\"Return True if the X is a pandas dataframe or series.\"\"\"\n2325 |     try:\n2326 |         pd = sys.modules[\"pandas\"]\n2327 |     except KeyError:\n2328 |         return False\n2329 |     return isinstance(X, (pd.DataFrame, pd.Series))\n2330 | \n2331 | \n2332 | def _is_pandas_df(X):\n2333 |     \"\"\"Return True if the X is a pandas dataframe.\"\"\"\n2334 |     try:\n2335 |         pd = sys.modules[\"pandas\"]\n2336 |     except KeyError:\n2337 |         return False\n2338 |     return isinstance(X, pd.DataFrame)\n2339 | \n2340 | \n2341 | def _is_polars_df_or_series(X):\n2342 |     \"\"\"Return True if the X is a polars dataframe or series.\"\"\"\n2343 |     try:\n2344 |         pl = sys.modules[\"polars\"]\n2345 |     except KeyError:\n2346 |         return False\n2347 |     return isinstance(X, (pl.DataFrame, pl.Series))\n2348 | \n2349 | \n2350 | def _is_polars_df(X):\n2351 |     \"\"\"Return True if the X is a polars dataframe.\"\"\"\n2352 |     try:\n2353 |         pl = sys.modules[\"polars\"]\n2354 |     except KeyError:\n2355 |         return False\n2356 |     return isinstance(X, pl.DataFrame)\n2357 | \n2358 | \n2359 | def _get_feature_names(X):\n2360 |     \"\"\"Get feature names from X.\n2361 | \n2362 |     Support for other array containers should place its implementation here.\n2363 | \n2364 |     Parameters\n2365 |     ----------\n2366 |     X : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n2367 |         Array container to extract feature names.\n2368 | \n2369 |         - pandas dataframe : The columns will be considered to be feature\n2370 |           names. If the dataframe contains non-string feature names, `None` is\n2371 |           returned.\n2372 |         - All other array containers will return `None`.\n2373 | \n2374 |     Returns\n2375 |     -------\n2376 |     names: ndarray or None\n2377 |         Feature names of `X`. Unrecognized array containers will return `None`.\n2378 |     \"\"\"\n2379 |     feature_names = None\n2380 | \n2381 |     # extract feature names for support array containers\n2382 |     if _is_pandas_df(X):\n2383 |         # Make sure we can inspect columns names from pandas, even with\n2384 |         # versions too old to expose a working implementation of\n2385 |         # __dataframe__.column_names() and avoid introducing any\n2386 |         # additional copy.\n2387 |         # TODO: remove the pandas-specific branch once the minimum supported\n2388 |         # version of pandas has a working implementation of\n2389 |         # __dataframe__.column_names() that is guaranteed to not introduce any\n2390 |         # additional copy of the data without having to impose allow_copy=False\n2391 |         # that could fail with other libraries. Note: in the longer term, we\n2392 |         # could decide to instead rely on the __dataframe_namespace__ API once\n2393 |         # adopted by our minimally supported pandas version.\n2394 |         feature_names = np.asarray(X.columns, dtype=object)\n2395 |     elif hasattr(X, \"__dataframe__\"):\n2396 |         df_protocol = X.__dataframe__()\n2397 |         feature_names = np.asarray(list(df_protocol.column_names()), dtype=object)\n2398 | \n2399 |     if feature_names is None or len(feature_names) == 0:\n2400 |         return\n2401 | \n2402 |     types = sorted(t.__qualname__ for t in set(type(v) for v in feature_names))\n2403 | \n2404 |     # mixed type of string and non-string is not supported\n2405 |     if len(types) > 1 and \"str\" in types:\n2406 |         raise TypeError(\n2407 |             \"Feature names are only supported if all input features have string names, \"\n2408 |             f\"but your input has {types} as feature name / column name types. \"\n2409 |             \"If you want feature names to be stored and validated, you must convert \"\n2410 |             \"them all to strings, by using X.columns = X.columns.astype(str) for \"\n2411 |             \"example. Otherwise you can remove feature / column names from your input \"\n2412 |             \"data, or convert them all to a non-string data type.\"\n2413 |         )\n2414 | \n2415 |     # Only feature names of all strings are supported\n2416 |     if len(types) == 1 and types[0] == \"str\":\n2417 |         return feature_names\n2418 | \n2419 | \n2420 | def _check_feature_names_in(estimator, input_features=None, *, generate_names=True):\n2421 |     \"\"\"Check `input_features` and generate names if needed.\n2422 | \n2423 |     Commonly used in :term:`get_feature_names_out`.\n2424 | \n2425 |     Parameters\n2426 |     ----------\n2427 |     input_features : array-like of str or None, default=None\n2428 |         Input features.\n2429 | \n2430 |         - If `input_features` is `None`, then `feature_names_in_` is\n2431 |           used as feature names in. If `feature_names_in_` is not defined,\n2432 |           then the following input feature names are generated:\n2433 |           `[\"x0\", \"x1\", ..., \"x(n_features_in_ - 1)\"]`.\n2434 |         - If `input_features` is an array-like, then `input_features` must\n2435 |           match `feature_names_in_` if `feature_names_in_` is defined.\n2436 | \n2437 |     generate_names : bool, default=True\n2438 |         Whether to generate names when `input_features` is `None` and\n2439 |         `estimator.feature_names_in_` is not defined. This is useful for transformers\n2440 |         that validates `input_features` but do not require them in\n2441 |         :term:`get_feature_names_out` e.g. `PCA`.\n2442 | \n2443 |     Returns\n2444 |     -------\n2445 |     feature_names_in : ndarray of str or `None`\n2446 |         Feature names in.\n2447 |     \"\"\"\n2448 | \n2449 |     feature_names_in_ = getattr(estimator, \"feature_names_in_\", None)\n2450 |     n_features_in_ = getattr(estimator, \"n_features_in_\", None)\n2451 | \n2452 |     if input_features is not None:\n2453 |         input_features = np.asarray(input_features, dtype=object)\n2454 |         if feature_names_in_ is not None and not np.array_equal(\n2455 |             feature_names_in_, input_features\n2456 |         ):\n2457 |             raise ValueError(\"input_features is not equal to feature_names_in_\")\n2458 | \n2459 |         if n_features_in_ is not None and len(input_features) != n_features_in_:\n2460 |             raise ValueError(\n2461 |                 \"input_features should have length equal to number of \"\n2462 |                 f\"features ({n_features_in_}), got {len(input_features)}\"\n2463 |             )\n2464 |         return input_features\n2465 | \n2466 |     if feature_names_in_ is not None:\n2467 |         return feature_names_in_\n2468 | \n2469 |     if not generate_names:\n2470 |         return\n2471 | \n2472 |     # Generates feature names if `n_features_in_` is defined\n2473 |     if n_features_in_ is None:\n2474 |         raise ValueError(\"Unable to generate feature names without n_features_in_\")\n2475 | \n2476 |     return np.asarray([f\"x{i}\" for i in range(n_features_in_)], dtype=object)\n2477 | \n2478 | \n2479 | def _generate_get_feature_names_out(estimator, n_features_out, input_features=None):\n2480 |     \"\"\"Generate feature names out for estimator using the estimator name as the prefix.\n2481 | \n2482 |     The input_feature names are validated but not used. This function is useful\n2483 |     for estimators that generate their own names based on `n_features_out`, i.e. PCA.\n2484 | \n2485 |     Parameters\n2486 |     ----------\n2487 |     estimator : estimator instance\n2488 |         Estimator producing output feature names.\n2489 | \n2490 |     n_feature_out : int\n2491 |         Number of feature names out.\n2492 | \n2493 |     input_features : array-like of str or None, default=None\n2494 |         Only used to validate feature names with `estimator.feature_names_in_`.\n2495 | \n2496 |     Returns\n2497 |     -------\n2498 |     feature_names_in : ndarray of str or `None`\n2499 |         Feature names in.\n2500 |     \"\"\"\n2501 |     _check_feature_names_in(estimator, input_features, generate_names=False)\n2502 |     estimator_name = estimator.__class__.__name__.lower()\n2503 |     return np.asarray(\n2504 |         [f\"{estimator_name}{i}\" for i in range(n_features_out)], dtype=object\n2505 |     )\n2506 | \n2507 | \n2508 | def _check_monotonic_cst(estimator, monotonic_cst=None):\n2509 |     \"\"\"Check the monotonic constraints and return the corresponding array.\n2510 | \n2511 |     This helper function should be used in the `fit` method of an estimator\n2512 |     that supports monotonic constraints and called after the estimator has\n2513 |     introspected input data to set the `n_features_in_` and optionally the\n2514 |     `feature_names_in_` attributes.\n2515 | \n2516 |     .. versionadded:: 1.2\n2517 | \n2518 |     Parameters\n2519 |     ----------\n2520 |     estimator : estimator instance\n2521 | \n2522 |     monotonic_cst : array-like of int, dict of str or None, default=None\n2523 |         Monotonic constraints for the features.\n2524 | \n2525 |         - If array-like, then it should contain only -1, 0 or 1. Each value\n2526 |             will be checked to be in [-1, 0, 1]. If a value is -1, then the\n2527 |             corresponding feature is required to be monotonically decreasing.\n2528 |         - If dict, then it the keys should be the feature names occurring in\n2529 |             `estimator.feature_names_in_` and the values should be -1, 0 or 1.\n2530 |         - If None, then an array of 0s will be allocated.\n2531 | \n2532 |     Returns\n2533 |     -------\n2534 |     monotonic_cst : ndarray of int\n2535 |         Monotonic constraints for each feature.\n2536 |     \"\"\"\n2537 |     original_monotonic_cst = monotonic_cst\n2538 |     if monotonic_cst is None or isinstance(monotonic_cst, dict):\n2539 |         monotonic_cst = np.full(\n2540 |             shape=estimator.n_features_in_,\n2541 |             fill_value=0,\n2542 |             dtype=np.int8,\n2543 |         )\n2544 |         if isinstance(original_monotonic_cst, dict):\n2545 |             if not hasattr(estimator, \"feature_names_in_\"):\n2546 |                 raise ValueError(\n2547 |                     f\"{estimator.__class__.__name__} was not fitted on data \"\n2548 |                     \"with feature names. Pass monotonic_cst as an integer \"\n2549 |                     \"array instead.\"\n2550 |                 )\n2551 |             unexpected_feature_names = list(\n2552 |                 set(original_monotonic_cst) - set(estimator.feature_names_in_)\n2553 |             )\n2554 |             unexpected_feature_names.sort()  # deterministic error message\n2555 |             n_unexpeced = len(unexpected_feature_names)\n2556 |             if unexpected_feature_names:\n2557 |                 if len(unexpected_feature_names) > 5:\n2558 |                     unexpected_feature_names = unexpected_feature_names[:5]\n2559 |                     unexpected_feature_names.append(\"...\")\n2560 |                 raise ValueError(\n2561 |                     f\"monotonic_cst contains {n_unexpeced} unexpected feature \"\n2562 |                     f\"names: {unexpected_feature_names}.\"\n2563 |                 )\n2564 |             for feature_idx, feature_name in enumerate(estimator.feature_names_in_):\n2565 |                 if feature_name in original_monotonic_cst:\n2566 |                     cst = original_monotonic_cst[feature_name]\n2567 |                     if cst not in [-1, 0, 1]:\n2568 |                         raise ValueError(\n2569 |                             f\"monotonic_cst['{feature_name}'] must be either \"\n2570 |                             f\"-1, 0 or 1. Got {cst!r}.\"\n2571 |                         )\n2572 |                     monotonic_cst[feature_idx] = cst\n2573 |     else:\n2574 |         unexpected_cst = np.setdiff1d(monotonic_cst, [-1, 0, 1])\n2575 |         if unexpected_cst.shape[0]:\n2576 |             raise ValueError(\n2577 |                 \"monotonic_cst must be an array-like of -1, 0 or 1. Observed \"\n2578 |                 f\"values: {unexpected_cst.tolist()}.\"\n2579 |             )\n2580 | \n2581 |         monotonic_cst = np.asarray(monotonic_cst, dtype=np.int8)\n2582 |         if monotonic_cst.shape[0] != estimator.n_features_in_:\n2583 |             raise ValueError(\n2584 |                 f\"monotonic_cst has shape {monotonic_cst.shape} but the input data \"\n2585 |                 f\"X has {estimator.n_features_in_} features.\"\n2586 |             )\n2587 |     return monotonic_cst\n2588 | \n2589 | \n2590 | def _check_pos_label_consistency(pos_label, y_true):\n2591 |     \"\"\"Check if `pos_label` need to be specified or not.\n2592 | \n2593 |     In binary classification, we fix `pos_label=1` if the labels are in the set\n2594 |     {-1, 1} or {0, 1}. Otherwise, we raise an error asking to specify the\n2595 |     `pos_label` parameters.\n2596 | \n2597 |     Parameters\n2598 |     ----------\n2599 |     pos_label : int, float, bool, str or None\n2600 |         The positive label.\n2601 |     y_true : ndarray of shape (n_samples,)\n2602 |         The target vector.\n2603 | \n2604 |     Returns\n2605 |     -------\n2606 |     pos_label : int, float, bool or str\n2607 |         If `pos_label` can be inferred, it will be returned.\n2608 | \n2609 |     Raises\n2610 |     ------\n2611 |     ValueError\n2612 |         In the case that `y_true` does not have label in {-1, 1} or {0, 1},\n2613 |         it will raise a `ValueError`.\n2614 |     \"\"\"\n2615 |     # ensure binary classification if pos_label is not specified\n2616 |     # classes.dtype.kind in ('O', 'U', 'S') is required to avoid\n2617 |     # triggering a FutureWarning by calling np.array_equal(a, b)\n2618 |     # when elements in the two arrays are not comparable.\n2619 |     if pos_label is None:\n2620 |         # Compute classes only if pos_label is not specified:\n2621 |         classes = np.unique(y_true)\n2622 |         if classes.dtype.kind in \"OUS\" or not (\n2623 |             np.array_equal(classes, [0, 1])\n2624 |             or np.array_equal(classes, [-1, 1])\n2625 |             or np.array_equal(classes, [0])\n2626 |             or np.array_equal(classes, [-1])\n2627 |             or np.array_equal(classes, [1])\n2628 |         ):\n2629 |             classes_repr = \", \".join([repr(c) for c in classes.tolist()])\n2630 |             raise ValueError(\n2631 |                 f\"y_true takes value in {{{classes_repr}}} and pos_label is not \"\n2632 |                 \"specified: either make y_true take value in {0, 1} or \"\n2633 |                 \"{-1, 1} or pass pos_label explicitly.\"\n2634 |             )\n2635 |         pos_label = 1\n2636 | \n2637 |     return pos_label\n2638 | \n2639 | \n2640 | def _to_object_array(sequence):\n2641 |     \"\"\"Convert sequence to a 1-D NumPy array of object dtype.\n2642 | \n2643 |     numpy.array constructor has a similar use but it's output\n2644 |     is ambiguous. It can be 1-D NumPy array of object dtype if\n2645 |     the input is a ragged array, but if the input is a list of\n2646 |     equal length arrays, then the output is a 2D numpy.array.\n2647 |     _to_object_array solves this ambiguity by guarantying that\n2648 |     the output is a 1-D NumPy array of objects for any input.\n2649 | \n2650 |     Parameters\n2651 |     ----------\n2652 |     sequence : array-like of shape (n_elements,)\n2653 |         The sequence to be converted.\n2654 | \n2655 |     Returns\n2656 |     -------\n2657 |     out : ndarray of shape (n_elements,), dtype=object\n2658 |         The converted sequence into a 1-D NumPy array of object dtype.\n2659 | \n2660 |     Examples\n2661 |     --------\n2662 |     >>> import numpy as np\n2663 |     >>> from sklearn.utils.validation import _to_object_array\n2664 |     >>> _to_object_array([np.array([0]), np.array([1])])\n2665 |     array([array([0]), array([1])], dtype=object)\n2666 |     >>> _to_object_array([np.array([0]), np.array([1, 2])])\n2667 |     array([array([0]), array([1, 2])], dtype=object)\n2668 |     >>> _to_object_array([np.array([0]), np.array([1, 2])])\n2669 |     array([array([0]), array([1, 2])], dtype=object)\n2670 |     \"\"\"\n2671 |     out = np.empty(len(sequence), dtype=object)\n2672 |     out[:] = sequence\n2673 |     return out\n2674 | \n2675 | \n2676 | def _check_feature_names(estimator, X, *, reset):\n2677 |     \"\"\"Set or check the `feature_names_in_` attribute of an estimator.\n2678 | \n2679 |     .. versionadded:: 1.0\n2680 | \n2681 |     .. versionchanged:: 1.6\n2682 |         Moved from :class:`~sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` to\n2683 |         :mod:`sklearn.utils.validation`.\n2684 | \n2685 |     Parameters\n2686 |     ----------\n2687 |     estimator : estimator instance\n2688 |         The estimator to validate the input for.\n2689 | \n2690 |     X : {ndarray, dataframe} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n2691 |         The input samples.\n2692 | \n2693 |     reset : bool\n2694 |         Whether to reset the `feature_names_in_` attribute.\n2695 |         If False, the input will be checked for consistency with\n2696 |         feature names of data provided when reset was last True.\n2697 |         .. note::\n2698 |            It is recommended to call `reset=True` in `fit` and in the first\n2699 |            call to `partial_fit`. All other methods that validate `X`\n2700 |            should set `reset=False`.\n2701 |     \"\"\"\n2702 | \n2703 |     if reset:\n2704 |         feature_names_in = _get_feature_names(X)\n2705 |         if feature_names_in is not None:\n2706 |             estimator.feature_names_in_ = feature_names_in\n2707 |         elif hasattr(estimator, \"feature_names_in_\"):\n2708 |             # Delete the attribute when the estimator is fitted on a new dataset\n2709 |             # that has no feature names.\n2710 |             delattr(estimator, \"feature_names_in_\")\n2711 |         return\n2712 | \n2713 |     fitted_feature_names = getattr(estimator, \"feature_names_in_\", None)\n2714 |     X_feature_names = _get_feature_names(X)\n2715 | \n2716 |     if fitted_feature_names is None and X_feature_names is None:\n2717 |         # no feature names seen in fit and in X\n2718 |         return\n2719 | \n2720 |     if X_feature_names is not None and fitted_feature_names is None:\n2721 |         warnings.warn(\n2722 |             f\"X has feature names, but {estimator.__class__.__name__} was fitted \"\n2723 |             \"without feature names\"\n2724 |         )\n2725 |         return\n2726 | \n2727 |     if X_feature_names is None and fitted_feature_names is not None:\n2728 |         warnings.warn(\n2729 |             \"X does not have valid feature names, but\"\n2730 |             f\" {estimator.__class__.__name__} was fitted with feature names\"\n2731 |         )\n2732 |         return\n2733 | \n2734 |     # validate the feature names against the `feature_names_in_` attribute\n2735 |     if len(fitted_feature_names) != len(X_feature_names) or np.any(\n2736 |         fitted_feature_names != X_feature_names\n2737 |     ):\n2738 |         message = \"The feature names should match those that were passed during fit.\\n\"\n2739 |         fitted_feature_names_set = set(fitted_feature_names)\n2740 |         X_feature_names_set = set(X_feature_names)\n2741 | \n2742 |         unexpected_names = sorted(X_feature_names_set - fitted_feature_names_set)\n2743 |         missing_names = sorted(fitted_feature_names_set - X_feature_names_set)\n2744 | \n2745 |         def add_names(names):\n2746 |             output = \"\"\n2747 |             max_n_names = 5\n2748 |             for i, name in enumerate(names):\n2749 |                 if i >= max_n_names:\n2750 |                     output += \"- ...\\n\"\n2751 |                     break\n2752 |                 output += f\"- {name}\\n\"\n2753 |             return output\n2754 | \n2755 |         if unexpected_names:\n2756 |             message += \"Feature names unseen at fit time:\\n\"\n2757 |             message += add_names(unexpected_names)\n2758 | \n2759 |         if missing_names:\n2760 |             message += \"Feature names seen at fit time, yet now missing:\\n\"\n2761 |             message += add_names(missing_names)\n2762 | \n2763 |         if not missing_names and not unexpected_names:\n2764 |             message += \"Feature names must be in the same order as they were in fit.\\n\"\n2765 | \n2766 |         raise ValueError(message)\n2767 | \n2768 | \n2769 | def _check_n_features(estimator, X, reset):\n2770 |     \"\"\"Set the `n_features_in_` attribute, or check against it on an estimator.\n2771 | \n2772 |     .. versionchanged:: 1.6\n2773 |         Moved from :class:`~sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` to\n2774 |         :mod:`~sklearn.utils.validation`.\n2775 | \n2776 |     Parameters\n2777 |     ----------\n2778 |     estimator : estimator instance\n2779 |         The estimator to validate the input for.\n2780 | \n2781 |     X : {ndarray, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n2782 |         The input samples.\n2783 | \n2784 |     reset : bool\n2785 |         If True, the `n_features_in_` attribute is set to `X.shape[1]`.\n2786 |         If False and the attribute exists, then check that it is equal to\n2787 |         `X.shape[1]`. If False and the attribute does *not* exist, then\n2788 |         the check is skipped.\n2789 |         .. note::\n2790 |            It is recommended to call reset=True in `fit` and in the first\n2791 |            call to `partial_fit`. All other methods that validate `X`\n2792 |            should set `reset=False`.\n2793 |     \"\"\"\n2794 |     try:\n2795 |         n_features = _num_features(X)\n2796 |     except TypeError as e:\n2797 |         if not reset and hasattr(estimator, \"n_features_in_\"):\n2798 |             raise ValueError(\n2799 |                 \"X does not contain any features, but \"\n2800 |                 f\"{estimator.__class__.__name__} is expecting \"\n2801 |                 f\"{estimator.n_features_in_} features\"\n2802 |             ) from e\n2803 |         # If the number of features is not defined and reset=True,\n2804 |         # then we skip this check\n2805 |         return\n2806 | \n2807 |     if reset:\n2808 |         estimator.n_features_in_ = n_features\n2809 |         return\n2810 | \n2811 |     if not hasattr(estimator, \"n_features_in_\"):\n2812 |         # Skip this check if the expected number of expected input features\n2813 |         # was not recorded by calling fit first. This is typically the case\n2814 |         # for stateless transformers.\n2815 |         return\n2816 | \n2817 |     if n_features != estimator.n_features_in_:\n2818 |         raise ValueError(\n2819 |             f\"X has {n_features} features, but {estimator.__class__.__name__} \"\n2820 |             f\"is expecting {estimator.n_features_in_} features as input.\"\n2821 |         )\n2822 | \n2823 | \n2824 | def validate_data(\n2825 |     _estimator,\n2826 |     /,\n2827 |     X=\"no_validation\",\n2828 |     y=\"no_validation\",\n2829 |     reset=True,\n2830 |     validate_separately=False,\n2831 |     skip_check_array=False,\n2832 |     **check_params,\n2833 | ):\n2834 |     \"\"\"Validate input data and set or check feature names and counts of the input.\n2835 | \n2836 |     This helper function should be used in an estimator that requires input\n2837 |     validation. This mutates the estimator and sets the `n_features_in_` and\n2838 |     `feature_names_in_` attributes if `reset=True`.\n2839 | \n2840 |     .. versionadded:: 1.6\n2841 | \n2842 |     Parameters\n2843 |     ----------\n2844 |     _estimator : estimator instance\n2845 |         The estimator to validate the input for.\n2846 | \n2847 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix, dataframe} of shape \\\n2848 |             (n_samples, n_features), default='no validation'\n2849 |         The input samples.\n2850 |         If `'no_validation'`, no validation is performed on `X`. This is\n2851 |         useful for meta-estimator which can delegate input validation to\n2852 |         their underlying estimator(s). In that case `y` must be passed and\n2853 |         the only accepted `check_params` are `multi_output` and\n2854 |         `y_numeric`.\n2855 | \n2856 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default='no_validation'\n2857 |         The targets.\n2858 | \n2859 |         - If `None`, :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array` is called on `X`. If\n2860 |           the estimator's `requires_y` tag is True, then an error will be raised.\n2861 |         - If `'no_validation'`, :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array` is called\n2862 |           on `X` and the estimator's `requires_y` tag is ignored. This is a default\n2863 |           placeholder and is never meant to be explicitly set. In that case `X` must be\n2864 |           passed.\n2865 |         - Otherwise, only `y` with `_check_y` or both `X` and `y` are checked with\n2866 |           either :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array` or\n2867 |           :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_X_y` depending on `validate_separately`.\n2868 | \n2869 |     reset : bool, default=True\n2870 |         Whether to reset the `n_features_in_` attribute.\n2871 |         If False, the input will be checked for consistency with data\n2872 |         provided when reset was last True.\n2873 | \n2874 |         .. note::\n2875 | \n2876 |            It is recommended to call `reset=True` in `fit` and in the first\n2877 |            call to `partial_fit`. All other methods that validate `X`\n2878 |            should set `reset=False`.\n2879 | \n2880 |     validate_separately : False or tuple of dicts, default=False\n2881 |         Only used if `y` is not `None`.\n2882 |         If `False`, call :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_X_y`. Else, it must be a tuple of\n2883 |         kwargs to be used for calling :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array` on `X` and `y`\n2884 |         respectively.\n2885 | \n2886 |         `estimator=self` is automatically added to these dicts to generate\n2887 |         more informative error message in case of invalid input data.\n2888 | \n2889 |     skip_check_array : bool, default=False\n2890 |         If `True`, `X` and `y` are unchanged and only `feature_names_in_` and\n2891 |         `n_features_in_` are checked. Otherwise, :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array`\n2892 |         is called on `X` and `y`.\n2893 | \n2894 |     **check_params : kwargs\n2895 |         Parameters passed to :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_array` or\n2896 |         :func:`~sklearn.utils.check_X_y`. Ignored if validate_separately\n2897 |         is not False.\n2898 | \n2899 |         `estimator=self` is automatically added to these params to generate\n2900 |         more informative error message in case of invalid input data.\n2901 | \n2902 |     Returns\n2903 |     -------\n2904 |     out : {ndarray, sparse matrix} or tuple of these\n2905 |         The validated input. A tuple is returned if both `X` and `y` are\n2906 |         validated.\n2907 |     \"\"\"\n2908 |     _check_feature_names(_estimator, X, reset=reset)\n2909 |     tags = get_tags(_estimator)\n2910 |     if y is None and tags.target_tags.required:\n2911 |         raise ValueError(\n2912 |             f\"This {_estimator.__class__.__name__} estimator \"\n2913 |             \"requires y to be passed, but the target y is None.\"\n2914 |         )\n2915 | \n2916 |     no_val_X = isinstance(X, str) and X == \"no_validation\"\n2917 |     no_val_y = y is None or isinstance(y, str) and y == \"no_validation\"\n2918 | \n2919 |     if no_val_X and no_val_y:\n2920 |         raise ValueError(\"Validation should be done on X, y or both.\")\n2921 | \n2922 |     default_check_params = {\"estimator\": _estimator}\n2923 |     check_params = {**default_check_params, **check_params}\n2924 | \n2925 |     if skip_check_array:\n2926 |         if not no_val_X and no_val_y:\n2927 |             out = X\n2928 |         elif no_val_X and not no_val_y:\n2929 |             out = y\n2930 |         else:\n2931 |             out = X, y\n2932 |     elif not no_val_X and no_val_y:\n2933 |         out = check_array(X, input_name=\"X\", **check_params)\n2934 |     elif no_val_X and not no_val_y:\n2935 |         out = _check_y(y, **check_params)\n2936 |     else:\n2937 |         if validate_separately:\n2938 |             # We need this because some estimators validate X and y\n2939 |             # separately, and in general, separately calling check_array()\n2940 |             # on X and y isn't equivalent to just calling check_X_y()\n2941 |             # :(\n2942 |             check_X_params, check_y_params = validate_separately\n2943 |             if \"estimator\" not in check_X_params:\n2944 |                 check_X_params = {**default_check_params, **check_X_params}\n2945 |             X = check_array(X, input_name=\"X\", **check_X_params)\n2946 |             if \"estimator\" not in check_y_params:\n2947 |                 check_y_params = {**default_check_params, **check_y_params}\n2948 |             y = check_array(y, input_name=\"y\", **check_y_params)\n2949 |         else:\n2950 |             X, y = check_X_y(X, y, **check_params)\n2951 |         out = X, y\n2952 | \n2953 |     if not no_val_X and check_params.get(\"ensure_2d\", True):\n2954 |         _check_n_features(_estimator, X, reset=reset)\n2955 | \n2956 |     return out\n2957 | \n```\n"}, {"content": "Let me also check for related test files:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>['test_regression.py']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nNo results for test files: `test_regression.py`. Please do not search for any test files.\n"}, {"content": "Based on the analysis of the issue and the code, I can now provide the locations that need modification to implement the requested feature. Here's the output in the required format:\n\n```\nsklearn/metrics/_regression.py\nline: 58-137\nfunction: _check_reg_targets\n- This is where the core functionality needs to be modified to include dtype handling\n\nsklearn/utils/_array_api.py\nline: 673-695\nfunction: _find_matching_floating_dtype\n- Reference implementation for dtype handling logic\n\nsklearn/metrics/_regression.py\nline: ~97-114\nfunction: _check_reg_targets\n- Where the dtype parameter is currently handled and passed through\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 730-112\nfunction: check_array\n- Handles array validation and dtype processing\n\nsklearn/utils/_array_api.py\nline: 97-111\nfunction: get_namespace\n- Gets array namespace information used in dtype determination\n```\n\nThe main modifications would need to be made in `sklearn/metrics/_regression.py` to integrate the `_find_matching_floating_dtype` functionality directly into the `_check_reg_targets` function. The rest of the files provide the supporting functionality and validation logic that would be affected by these changes.\n\n<function=finish>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}], "tools": [{"type": "function", "function": {"name": "finish", "description": "Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task."}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "search_code_snippets", "description": "Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"search_terms": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search."}, "line_nums": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search."}, "file_path_or_pattern": {"type": "string", "description": "A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.", "default": "**/*.py"}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_entity_contents", "description": "\nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"entity_names": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py')."}}, "required": ["entity_names"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "explore_tree_structure", "description": "\nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"start_entities": {"description": "List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}}, "direction": {"description": "Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.", "type": "string", "enum": ["upstream", "downstream", "both"], "default": "downstream"}, "traversal_depth": {"description": "Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).", "type": "integer", "default": 2}, "entity_type_filter": {"description": "List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}, "dependency_type_filter": {"description": "List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.", "type": ["array", "null"], "items": {"type": "string"}, "default": null}}, "required": ["start_entities"]}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_artifacts", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads `DaLIC/vt_results/<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json` and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"artifact_type": {"type": "string", "enum": ["all", "function", "method", "class"], "default": "all", "description": "Optional exact filter for artifact definition type."}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.", "default": null}, "name_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 8, "description": "Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice."}}, "required": []}}}, {"type": "function", "function": {"name": "get_trace_data_dependencies", "description": "\nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\nThis tool reads `DaLIC/vt_results/<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json` and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {"symbol_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.", "default": null}, "scope_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.", "default": null}, "path_contains": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.", "default": null}, "edge_type": {"type": ["string", "null"], "description": "Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`.", "default": null}, "start": {"type": "integer", "default": 0, "description": "Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges."}, "limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 12, "description": "Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice."}, "path_limit": {"type": "integer", "default": 4, "description": "Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include."}}, "required": []}}}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 193268, "completion_tokens": 534}, "time": 94.40872597694397}]}}
{"instance_id": "scikit-learn__scikit-learn-30327", "found_files": [["sklearn/utils/_tags.py", "sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py", "sklearn/base.py", "sklearn/utils/validation.py"]], "found_modules": [["sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_estimator_tags_renamed", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:validate_data", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_InstancesOf", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_check_device_cpu", "sklearn/decomposition/_truncated_svd.py:TruncatedSVD", "sklearn/cluster/_birch.py:_CFSubcluster", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_accept_device_cpu", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:Version", "sklearn/mixture/_base.py:BaseMixture", "sklearn/feature_selection/_sequential.py:SequentialFeatureSelector", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:ArffException", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags", "doc/api_reference.py:_get_guide", "benchmarks/bench_plot_incremental_pca.py:plot_results", "examples/linear_model/plot_sgd_loss_functions.py:modified_huber_loss", "benchmarks/bench_isotonic.py:generate_perturbed_logarithm_dataset", "sklearn/utils/_estimator_html_repr.py:_get_css_style", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/svm.py:SVCBenchmark", "benchmarks/bench_glmnet.py:rmse", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/model_selection.py:CrossValidationBenchmark", "benchmarks/bench_plot_parallel_pairwise.py:euclidean_distances", "benchmarks/bench_plot_parallel_pairwise.py:rbf_kernels", "examples/text/plot_document_classification_20newsgroups.py:size_mb", "sklearn/metrics/_classification.py:hamming_loss", "sklearn/preprocessing/_data.py:QuantileTransformer"]], "found_entities": [["sklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py:check_estimator_tags_renamed", "sklearn/utils/validation.py:validate_data", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_InstancesOf.is_satisfied_by", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint.is_satisfied_by", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_check_device_cpu", "sklearn/utils/_param_validation.py:_NoneConstraint.__str__", "sklearn/decomposition/_truncated_svd.py:TruncatedSVD.__sklearn_tags__", "sklearn/cluster/_birch.py:_CFSubcluster.update", "sklearn/utils/_array_api.py:_accept_device_cpu", "sklearn/externals/_packaging/version.py:Version.__repr__", "sklearn/mixture/_base.py:BaseMixture._get_parameters", "sklearn/feature_selection/_sequential.py:SequentialFeatureSelector._get_support_mask", "sklearn/externals/_arff.py:ArffException.__str__", "sklearn/utils/_tags.py:get_tags", "doc/api_reference.py:_get_guide", "benchmarks/bench_plot_incremental_pca.py:plot_results", "examples/linear_model/plot_sgd_loss_functions.py:modified_huber_loss", "benchmarks/bench_isotonic.py:generate_perturbed_logarithm_dataset", "sklearn/utils/_estimator_html_repr.py:_get_css_style", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/svm.py:SVCBenchmark.make_scorers", "benchmarks/bench_glmnet.py:rmse", "asv_benchmarks/benchmarks/model_selection.py:CrossValidationBenchmark.track_crossval", "benchmarks/bench_plot_parallel_pairwise.py:euclidean_distances", "benchmarks/bench_plot_parallel_pairwise.py:rbf_kernels", "examples/text/plot_document_classification_20newsgroups.py:size_mb", "sklearn/metrics/_classification.py:hamming_loss", "sklearn/preprocessing/_data.py:QuantileTransformer.fit"]], "raw_output_loc": ["Based on the analysis of the issue and code exploration, here are the key locations that need modification to implement the feature request:\n\n```\nsklearn/utils/_tags.py\nline: 293-334\nfunction: get_tags\ndescription: Main function to convert tags between old and new formats\n\nsklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\nline: 4447-4466\nfunction: check_estimator_tags_renamed\ndescription: Function to check for deprecated _get_tags/_more_tags usage\n\nsklearn/base.py\nline: 1-50\nclass: BaseEstimator\ndescription: Contains the _get_tags implementation and related functionality\n\nsklearn/utils/validation.py\nline: 2824-2956\nfunction: validate_data\ndescription: Uses get_tags to validate estimator tags\n\nsklearn/utils/estimator_checks.py\nline: 4447-4466\nfunction: check_estimator_tags_renamed\ndescription: Critical validation logic for deprecated tag system\n```\n\nThe key components identified from the code exploration are:\n\n1. The main tagging system implementation is in `utils/_tags.py` where the `get_tags` function exists to handle tag conversions.\n2. There's already a mechanism for checking deprecated tag systems in `utils/estimator_checks.py` through `check_estimator_tags_renamed`.\n3. The base estimator class defines the core tag-related functionality.\n4. Several parts of the codebase depend on these tags for validation and metadata retrieval.\n\nThese locations form the core areas that need to be considered for implementing the feature request, which involves:\n- Warning users when deprecated tag systems are used\n- Providing compatibility for both old and new tag systems\n- Deprecating certain tag-related functions\n- Ensuring proper warnings and error handling"], "meta_data": {"repo": "scikit-learn/scikit-learn", "base_commit": "90a51c12d6159f72ab8990ea1a3ff835861ec81d", "problem_statement": "MAINT conversion old->new/new->old tags\nTowards #30298 \r\n\r\nThis PR provides a way to convert old tags into new tags and new tags into old tags.\r\nWe should make sure that:\r\n\r\n- An estimator implementing `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` get a warning but that the check_estimator should be working reasonably\r\n- `_safe_tags` should raise a deprecation warning\r\n- we should make sure that `get_tags` can get old tags and convert it to new tags. The tricky part is to detect whether `__sklearn_tags__` if present is implemented by the `BaseEstimator` from scikit-learn or the child class. If this is only the `BaseEstimator`, we should raise a warning to move to the new API and we should temporary use the `_get_tags`/`_safe_tags`, otherwise we are fine.\r\n\n", "patch": "diff --git a/sklearn/base.py b/sklearn/base.py\nindex d646f8d3e56bf..2c82cf05a6c5a 100644\n--- a/sklearn/base.py\n+++ b/sklearn/base.py\n@@ -389,6 +389,33 @@ def __setstate__(self, state):\n         except AttributeError:\n             self.__dict__.update(state)\n \n+    # TODO(1.7): Remove this method\n+    def _more_tags(self):\n+        \"\"\"This code should never be reached since our `get_tags` will fallback on\n+        `__sklearn_tags__` implemented below. We keep it for backward compatibility.\n+        It is tested in `test_base_estimator_more_tags` in\n+        `sklearn/utils/testing/test_tags.py`.\"\"\"\n+        from sklearn.utils._tags import _to_old_tags, default_tags\n+\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            \"The `_more_tags` method is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+            \"1.7. Please implement the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+        return _to_old_tags(default_tags(self))\n+\n+    # TODO(1.7): Remove this method\n+    def _get_tags(self):\n+        from sklearn.utils._tags import _to_old_tags, get_tags\n+\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            \"The `_get_tags` method is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+            \"1.7. Please implement the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+\n+        return _to_old_tags(get_tags(self))\n+\n     def __sklearn_tags__(self):\n         return Tags(\n             estimator_type=None,\ndiff --git a/sklearn/utils/_tags.py b/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\nindex ccbc9d2438268..1ba1913c37234 100644\n--- a/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\n+++ b/sklearn/utils/_tags.py\n@@ -1,7 +1,9 @@\n from __future__ import annotations\n \n import warnings\n+from collections import OrderedDict\n from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n+from itertools import chain\n \n from .fixes import _dataclass_args\n \n@@ -290,6 +292,71 @@ def default_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n     )\n \n \n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _find_tags_provider(estimator, warn=True):\n+    \"\"\"Find the tags provider for an estimator.\n+\n+    Parameters\n+    ----------\n+    estimator : estimator object\n+        The estimator to find the tags provider for.\n+\n+    warn : bool, default=True\n+        Whether to warn if the tags provider is not found.\n+\n+    Returns\n+    -------\n+    tag_provider : str\n+        The tags provider for the estimator. Can be one of:\n+        - \"_get_tags\": to use the old tags infrastructure\n+        - \"__sklearn_tags__\": to use the new tags infrastructure\n+    \"\"\"\n+    mro_model = type(estimator).mro()\n+    tags_mro = OrderedDict()\n+    for klass in mro_model:\n+        tags_provider = []\n+        if \"_more_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"_more_tags\")\n+        if \"_get_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"_get_tags\")\n+        if \"__sklearn_tags__\" in vars(klass):\n+            tags_provider.append(\"__sklearn_tags__\")\n+        tags_mro[klass.__name__] = tags_provider\n+\n+    all_providers = set(chain.from_iterable(tags_mro.values()))\n+    if \"__sklearn_tags__\" not in all_providers:\n+        # default on the old tags infrastructure\n+        return \"_get_tags\"\n+\n+    tag_provider = \"__sklearn_tags__\"\n+    for klass in tags_mro:\n+        has_get_or_more_tags = any(\n+            provider in tags_mro[klass] for provider in (\"_get_tags\", \"_more_tags\")\n+        )\n+        has_sklearn_tags = \"__sklearn_tags__\" in tags_mro[klass]\n+\n+        if tags_mro[klass] and tag_provider == \"__sklearn_tags__\":  # is it empty\n+            if has_get_or_more_tags and not has_sklearn_tags:\n+                # Case where a class does not implement __sklearn_tags__ and we fallback\n+                # to _get_tags. We should therefore warn for implementing\n+                # __sklearn_tags__.\n+                tag_provider = \"_get_tags\"\n+                break\n+\n+    if warn and tag_provider == \"_get_tags\":\n+        warnings.warn(\n+            f\"The {estimator.__class__.__name__} or classes from which it inherits \"\n+            \"use `_get_tags` and `_more_tags`. Please define the \"\n+            \"`__sklearn_tags__` method, or inherit from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` \"\n+            \"and/or other appropriate mixins such as `sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, \"\n+            \"`sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, `sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and \"\n+            \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`. From scikit-learn 1.7, not defining \"\n+            \"`__sklearn_tags__` will raise an error.\",\n+            category=FutureWarning,\n+        )\n+    return tag_provider\n+\n+\n def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n     \"\"\"Get estimator tags.\n \n@@ -316,19 +383,201 @@ def get_tags(estimator) -> Tags:\n         The estimator tags.\n     \"\"\"\n \n-    if hasattr(estimator, \"__sklearn_tags__\"):\n+    tag_provider = _find_tags_provider(estimator)\n+\n+    if tag_provider == \"__sklearn_tags__\":\n         tags = estimator.__sklearn_tags__()\n     else:\n-        warnings.warn(\n-            f\"Estimator {estimator} has no __sklearn_tags__ attribute, which is \"\n-            \"defined in `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator`. This will raise an error in \"\n-            \"scikit-learn 1.8. Please define the __sklearn_tags__ method, or inherit \"\n-            \"from `sklearn.base.BaseEstimator` and other appropriate mixins such as \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.TransformerMixin`, `sklearn.base.ClassifierMixin`, \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.RegressorMixin`, and `sklearn.base.ClusterMixin`, and \"\n-            \"`sklearn.base.OutlierMixin`.\",\n-            category=FutureWarning,\n+        # TODO(1.7): Remove this branch of the code\n+        # Let's go through the MRO and patch each class implementing _more_tags\n+        sklearn_tags_provider = {}\n+        more_tags_provider = {}\n+        class_order = []\n+        for klass in reversed(type(estimator).mro()):\n+            if \"__sklearn_tags__\" in vars(klass):\n+                sklearn_tags_provider[klass] = klass.__sklearn_tags__(estimator)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n+                class_order.append(klass)\n+            elif \"_more_tags\" in vars(klass):\n+                more_tags_provider[klass] = klass._more_tags(estimator)  # type: ignore[attr-defined]\n+                class_order.append(klass)\n+\n+        # Find differences between consecutive in the case of __sklearn_tags__\n+        # inheritance\n+        sklearn_tags_diff = {}\n+        items = list(sklearn_tags_provider.items())\n+        for current_item, next_item in zip(items[:-1], items[1:]):\n+            current_name, current_tags = current_item\n+            next_name, next_tags = next_item\n+            current_tags = _to_old_tags(current_tags)\n+            next_tags = _to_old_tags(next_tags)\n+\n+            # Compare tags and store differences\n+            diff = {}\n+            for key in current_tags:\n+                if current_tags[key] != next_tags[key]:\n+                    diff[key] = next_tags[key]\n+\n+            sklearn_tags_diff[next_name] = diff\n+\n+        tags = {}\n+        for klass in class_order:\n+            if klass in sklearn_tags_diff:\n+                tags.update(sklearn_tags_diff[klass])\n+            elif klass in more_tags_provider:\n+                tags.update(more_tags_provider[klass])\n+\n+        tags = _to_new_tags(\n+            {**_to_old_tags(default_tags(estimator)), **tags}, estimator\n         )\n-        tags = default_tags(estimator)\n \n     return tags\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _safe_tags(estimator, key=None):\n+    warnings.warn(\n+        \"The `_safe_tags` function is deprecated in 1.6 and will be removed in \"\n+        \"1.7. Use the public `get_tags` function instead and make sure to implement \"\n+        \"the `__sklearn_tags__` method.\",\n+        category=FutureWarning,\n+    )\n+    tags = _to_old_tags(get_tags(estimator))\n+\n+    if key is not None:\n+        if key not in tags:\n+            raise ValueError(\n+                f\"The key {key} is not defined for the class \"\n+                f\"{estimator.__class__.__name__}.\"\n+            )\n+        return tags[key]\n+    return tags\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _to_new_tags(old_tags, estimator=None):\n+    \"\"\"Utility function convert old tags (dictionary) to new tags (dataclass).\"\"\"\n+    input_tags = InputTags(\n+        one_d_array=\"1darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        two_d_array=\"2darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        three_d_array=\"3darray\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        sparse=\"sparse\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        categorical=\"categorical\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        string=\"string\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        dict=\"dict\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        positive_only=old_tags[\"requires_positive_X\"],\n+        allow_nan=old_tags[\"allow_nan\"],\n+        pairwise=old_tags[\"pairwise\"],\n+    )\n+    target_tags = TargetTags(\n+        required=old_tags[\"requires_y\"],\n+        one_d_labels=\"1dlabels\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        two_d_labels=\"2dlabels\" in old_tags[\"X_types\"],\n+        positive_only=old_tags[\"requires_positive_y\"],\n+        multi_output=old_tags[\"multioutput\"] or old_tags[\"multioutput_only\"],\n+        single_output=not old_tags[\"multioutput_only\"],\n+    )\n+    if estimator is not None and (\n+        hasattr(estimator, \"transform\") or hasattr(estimator, \"fit_transform\")\n+    ):\n+        transformer_tags = TransformerTags(\n+            preserves_dtype=old_tags[\"preserves_dtype\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        transformer_tags = None\n+    estimator_type = getattr(estimator, \"_estimator_type\", None)\n+    if estimator_type == \"classifier\":\n+        classifier_tags = ClassifierTags(\n+            poor_score=old_tags[\"poor_score\"],\n+            multi_class=not old_tags[\"binary_only\"],\n+            multi_label=old_tags[\"multilabel\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        classifier_tags = None\n+    if estimator_type == \"regressor\":\n+        regressor_tags = RegressorTags(\n+            poor_score=old_tags[\"poor_score\"],\n+            multi_label=old_tags[\"multilabel\"],\n+        )\n+    else:\n+        regressor_tags = None\n+    return Tags(\n+        estimator_type=estimator_type,\n+        target_tags=target_tags,\n+        transformer_tags=transformer_tags,\n+        classifier_tags=classifier_tags,\n+        regressor_tags=regressor_tags,\n+        input_tags=input_tags,\n+        array_api_support=old_tags[\"array_api_support\"],\n+        no_validation=old_tags[\"no_validation\"],\n+        non_deterministic=old_tags[\"non_deterministic\"],\n+        requires_fit=old_tags[\"requires_fit\"],\n+        _skip_test=old_tags[\"_skip_test\"],\n+    )\n+\n+\n+# TODO(1.7): Remove this function\n+def _to_old_tags(new_tags):\n+    \"\"\"Utility function convert old tags (dictionary) to new tags (dataclass).\"\"\"\n+    if new_tags.classifier_tags:\n+        binary_only = not new_tags.classifier_tags.multi_class\n+        multilabel_clf = new_tags.classifier_tags.multi_label\n+        poor_score_clf = new_tags.classifier_tags.poor_score\n+    else:\n+        binary_only = False\n+        multilabel_clf = False\n+        poor_score_clf = False\n+\n+    if new_tags.regressor_tags:\n+        multilabel_reg = new_tags.regressor_tags.multi_label\n+        poor_score_reg = new_tags.regressor_tags.poor_score\n+    else:\n+        multilabel_reg = False\n+        poor_score_reg = False\n+\n+    if new_tags.transformer_tags:\n+        preserves_dtype = new_tags.transformer_tags.preserves_dtype\n+    else:\n+        preserves_dtype = [\"float64\"]\n+\n+    tags = {\n+        \"allow_nan\": new_tags.input_tags.allow_nan,\n+        \"array_api_support\": new_tags.array_api_support,\n+        \"binary_only\": binary_only,\n+        \"multilabel\": multilabel_clf or multilabel_reg,\n+        \"multioutput\": new_tags.target_tags.multi_output,\n+        \"multioutput_only\": (\n+            not new_tags.target_tags.single_output and new_tags.target_tags.multi_output\n+        ),\n+        \"no_validation\": new_tags.no_validation,\n+        \"non_deterministic\": new_tags.non_deterministic,\n+        \"pairwise\": new_tags.input_tags.pairwise,\n+        \"preserves_dtype\": preserves_dtype,\n+        \"poor_score\": poor_score_clf or poor_score_reg,\n+        \"requires_fit\": new_tags.requires_fit,\n+        \"requires_positive_X\": new_tags.input_tags.positive_only,\n+        \"requires_y\": new_tags.target_tags.required,\n+        \"requires_positive_y\": new_tags.target_tags.positive_only,\n+        \"_skip_test\": new_tags._skip_test,\n+        \"stateless\": new_tags.requires_fit,\n+    }\n+    X_types = []\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.one_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"1darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.two_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"2darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.three_d_array:\n+        X_types.append(\"3darray\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.sparse:\n+        X_types.append(\"sparse\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.categorical:\n+        X_types.append(\"categorical\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.string:\n+        X_types.append(\"string\")\n+    if new_tags.input_tags.dict:\n+        X_types.append(\"dict\")\n+    if new_tags.target_tags.one_d_labels:\n+        X_types.append(\"1dlabels\")\n+    if new_tags.target_tags.two_d_labels:\n+        X_types.append(\"2dlabels\")\n+    tags[\"X_types\"] = X_types\n+    return tags\n"}, "usage": {"cost($)": "0", "prompt_tokens": 107033, "completion_tokens": 592}, "loc_trajs": {"trajs": [{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "A chat between a curious user and an artificial intelligence assistant. The assistant gives helpful, detailed answers to the user's questions.\nThe assistant can use a Python environment with <execute_ipython>, e.g.:\n<execute_ipython>\nprint(\"Hello World!\")\n</execute_ipython>\n\nApart from the standard Python library, the assistant can also use the following functions (already imported) in <execute_ipython> environment:\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms: Optional[List[str]] = None, line_nums: Optional[List] = None, file_path_or_pattern: Optional[str] = '**/*.py') -> str:\n    Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n    This function supports retrieving the complete content of a code entity,\n    searching for code entities such as classes or functions by keywords, or locating specific lines within a file.\n    It also supports filtering searches based on a file path or file pattern.\n    Note:\n    1. If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n    - If a term is formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') ,\n    or just 'file_path', the corresponding complete code is retrieved or file content is retrieved.\n    - If a term matches a file, class, or function name, matched entities are retrieved.\n    - If there is no match with any module name, it attempts to find code snippets that likely contain the term.\n    2. If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets at the specified lines within the file defined by\n    `file_path_or_pattern`.\n    Args:\n    search_terms (Optional[List[str]]): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase.\n    Terms can be formatted as 'file_path:QualifiedName' to search for a specific module or entity within a file\n    (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum') or as 'file_path' to retrieve the complete content\n    of a file. This can also include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments.\n    line_nums (Optional[List[int]]): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file.\n    When provided, `file_path_or_pattern` must specify a valid file path.\n    file_path_or_pattern (Optional[str]): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results\n    to particular files or directories. Defaults to '**/*.py', meaning all Python files are searched by default.\n    If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n    Returns:\n    str: The search results, which may include code snippets, matching entities, or complete file content.\n    Example Usage:\n    # Search for the full content of a specific file\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py'])\n    # Search for a specific function\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n    # Search for specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\n    result = search_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n    # Combined search for a module name and within a specific file pattern\n    result = search_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"], file_path_or_pattern=\"src/**/*.py\")\n\nexplore_graph_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 1, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Args:\n    start_entities:\n    direction:\n    traversal_depth:\n    entity_type_filter:\n    dependency_type_filter:\n    Returns:\n\nexplore_tree_structure(start_entities: List[str], direction: str = 'downstream', traversal_depth: int = 2, entity_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None, dependency_type_filter: Optional[List[str]] = None):\n    Analyzes and displays the dependency structure around specified entities in a code graph.\n    This function searches and presents relationships and dependencies for the specified entities (such as classes, functions, files, or directories) in a code graph.\n    It explores how the input entities relate to others, using defined types of dependencies, including 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes' and 'inherits'.\n    The search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n    Example Usage:\n    1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n    direction='downstream',\n    traversal_depth=2,\n    entity_type_filter=['class', 'function'],\n    dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n    2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    get_local_structure(\n    start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n    direction='upstream',\n    traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n    Notes:\n    * Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n    * Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n    * Graph Context: The function operates on a pre-built code graph containing entities (e.g., files, classes and functions) and dependencies representing their interactions and relationships.\n    Parameters:\n    ----------\n    start_entities : list[str]\n    List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\n    - Entities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\"\n    (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\n    - For files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n    direction : str, optional\n    Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are:\n    - 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n    - 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others\n    (how others depend on them).\n    - 'both': Traversal in both directions.\n    Default is 'downstream'.\n    traversal_depth : int, optional\n    Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).\n    Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n    Default is 2.\n    entity_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all entity types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    dependency_type_filter : list[str], optional\n    List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal.\n    If None, all dependency types are included.\n    Default is None.\n    Returns:\n    -------\n    result : object\n    An object representing the traversal results, which includes discovered entities and their dependencies.\n\nget_trace_artifacts(artifact_type: str = 'all', path_contains: str | None = None, name_contains: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 8):\n    Return a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\n    This tool reads `unique_artifacts.json` from `DaLIC/vt_results/<instance_id>/`.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination,\n    then returns a compact JSON summary.\n    Tips:\n    - Use this once near the start of analyzing a new instance to inspect the raw execution-trace evidence.\n    - You can call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nget_trace_data_dependencies(symbol_contains: str | None = None, scope_contains: str | None = None, path_contains: str | None = None, edge_type: str | None = None, start: int = 0, limit: int = 12, path_limit: int = 4):\n    Return a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\n    This tool reads `unique_dep_tree.json` from `DaLIC/vt_results/<instance_id>/`.\n    It does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination,\n    then returns a compact JSON summary.\n    Tips:\n    - Use it when you need a precise dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n    - You can call it again at any time during analysis with narrower filters such as `scope_contains`, `symbol_contains`, or `edge_type`.\n\n\nFunction Usage Guidelines:\n- Issue Analysis and Retrieval: Begin by analyzing the issue and extracting relevant information for retrieval. Use the functions `search_in_repo` to gather more insights about the issue.\n- Accessing Files and Code: Use `get_file_structures` or `search_class_structures` to gain an overview of the files or classes; Call `get_file_content` to access the content of specific file relevant to the issue, and employ `search_class` or `search_method` to retrieve specific modules when you know the specific names of these modules.\n- Comprehensive Dependency Analysis: Prioritize analyzing the code dependencies of functions, methods, or classes mentioned in the problem description or those identified in relevant locations. Use `search_invoke_and_reference` as the primary tool to ensure all interactions within the codebase are captured.\n- Repository Overview: Use `get_repo_structure` to gain an overview of the repository. This aids in independently searching for related files or analyzing results from previous steps. However, this tool is relatively expensive, use other tools to search files, classes or methods if you know their potential names.\n- File Limitations: You may search as many files as necessary, but make sure not include noise.\n- Exclusions: Do not include any existing test case files in your findings.\n- Evaluate and prioritize findings from different search methods, reranking the answer when you give final output.\nIMPORTANT:\n- Parameter Accuracy: Ensure that the parameters used for each tool call are accurate, avoiding assumptions.\n\nResponses should be concise.\nThe assistant should attempt fewer things at a time instead of putting too many commands OR too much code in one \"execute\" block.\nInclude ONLY ONE <execute_ipython> per response, unless the assistant is finished with the task or needs more input or action from the user in order to proceed.\nIf the assistant is finished with the task you MUST include <finish></finish> in your response.\nIMPORTANT: Execute code using <execute_ipython> whenever possible.\nThe assistant should utilize full file paths to prevent path-related errors.\nThe assistant must avoid apologies and thanks in its responses.\nYou have access to the following functions:\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #1: finish ----\nDescription: Finish the interaction when the task is complete OR if the assistant cannot proceed further with the task.\nNo parameters are required for this function.\n---- END FUNCTION #1 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #2: search_code_snippets ----\nDescription: Searches the codebase to retrieve relevant code snippets based on given queries(terms or line numbers).\n** Note:\n- Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n- If `search_terms` are provided, it searches for code snippets based on each term:\n- If `line_nums` is provided, it searches for code snippets around the specified lines within the file defined by `file_path_or_pattern`.\n\n** Example Usage:\n# Search for code content contain keyword `order`, `bill`\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"order\", \"bill\"])\n\n# Search for a class\nsearch_code_snippets(search_terms=[\"MyClass\"])\n\n# Search for context around specific lines (10 and 15) within a file\nsearch_code_snippets(line_nums=[10, 15], file_path_or_pattern='src/example.py')\n\nParameters:\n  (1) search_terms (array, optional): A list of names, keywords, or code snippets to search for within the codebase. This can include potential function names, class names, or general code fragments. Either `search_terms` or `line_nums` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (2) line_nums (array, optional): Specific line numbers to locate code snippets within a specified file. Must be used alongside a valid `file_path_or_pattern`. Either `line_nums` or `search_terms` must be provided to perform a search.\n  (3) file_path_or_pattern (string, optional): A glob pattern or specific file path used to filter search results to particular files or directories. Defaults to \"**/*.py\", meaning all Python files are searched by default. If `line_nums` are provided, this must specify a specific file path.\n---- END FUNCTION #2 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #3: get_entity_contents ----\nDescription: \nSearches the codebase to retrieve the complete implementations of specified entities based on the provided entity names. \nThe tool can handle specific entity queries such as function names, class names, or file paths.\n\n**Usage Example:**\n# Search for a specific function implementation\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py:MyClass.func_name'])\n\n# Search for a file's complete content\nget_entity_contents(['src/my_file.py'])\n\n**Entity Name Format:**\n- To specify a function or class, use the format: `file_path:QualifiedName`\n  (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum').\n- To search for a file's content, use only the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n\nParameters:\n  (1) entity_names (array, required): A list of entity names to query. Each entity name can represent a function, class, or file. For functions or classes, the format should be 'file_path:QualifiedName' (e.g., 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'). For files, use just the file path (e.g., 'src/my_file.py').\n---- END FUNCTION #3 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #4: explore_tree_structure ----\nDescription: \nUnified repository exploring tool that traverses a pre-built code graph to retrieve dependency structure around specified entities.\nThe search can be controlled to traverse upstream (exploring dependencies that entities rely on) or downstream (exploring how entities impact others), with optional limits on traversal depth and filters for entity and dependency types.\n\nCode Graph Definition:\n* Entity Types: 'directory', 'file', 'class', 'function'.\n* Dependency Types: 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits'.\n* Hierarchy:\n    - Directories contain files and subdirectories.\n    - Files contain classes and functions.\n    - Classes contain inner classes and methods.\n    - Functions can contain inner functions.\n* Interactions:\n    - Files/classes/functions can import classes and functions.\n    - Classes can inherit from other classes.\n    - Classes and functions can invoke others (invocations in a class's `__init__` are attributed to the class).\nEntity ID:\n* Unique identifier including file path and module path.\n* Here's an example of an Entity ID: `\"interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func\"` identifies function `inner_func` within `method_a` of class `C` in `\"interface/C.py\"`.\n\nNotes:\n* Traversal Control: The `traversal_depth` parameter specifies how deep the function should explore the graph starting from the input entities.\n* Filtering: Use `entity_type_filter` and `dependency_type_filter` to narrow down the scope of the search, focusing on specific entity types and relationships.\n\n\nExample Usage:\n1. Exploring Outward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_a.py:ClassA'],\n        direction='downstream',\n        traversal_depth=2,\n        dependency_type_filter=['invokes', 'imports']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the dependencies of `ClassA` up to 2 levels deep, focusing only on classes and functions with 'invokes' and 'imports' relationships.\n\n2. Exploring Inward Dependencies:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=['src/module_b.py:FunctionY'],\n        direction='upstream',\n        traversal_depth=-1\n    )\n    ```\n    This finds all entities that depend on `FunctionY` without restricting the traversal depth.\n3. Exploring Repository Structure:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n      start_entities=['/'],\n      traversal_depth=2,\n      dependency_type_filter=['contains']\n    )\n    ```\n    This retrieves the tree repository structure from the root directory (/), traversing up to two levels deep and focusing only on 'contains' relationship.\n4. Generate Class Diagrams:\n    ```\n    explore_tree_structure(\n        start_entities=selected_entity_ids,\n        direction='both',\n        traverse_depth=-1,\n        dependency_type_filter=['inherits']\n    )\n    ```\n\nParameters:\n  (1) start_entities (array, required): List of entities (e.g., class, function, file, or directory paths) to begin the search from.\nEntities representing classes or functions must be formatted as \"file_path:QualifiedName\" (e.g., `interface/C.py:C.method_a.inner_func`).\nFor files or directories, provide only the file or directory path (e.g., `src/module_a.py` or `src/`).\n  (2) direction (string, optional): Direction of traversal in the code graph; allowed options are: `upstream`, `downstream`, `both`.\n- 'upstream': Traversal to explore dependencies that the specified entities rely on (how they depend on others).\n- 'downstream': Traversal to explore the effects or interactions of the specified entities on others (how others depend on them).\n- 'both': Traversal on both direction.\nAllowed values: [`upstream`, `downstream`, `both`]\n  (3) traversal_depth (integer, optional): Maximum depth of traversal. A value of -1 indicates unlimited depth (subject to a maximum limit).Must be either `-1` or a non-negative integer (\u2265 0).\n  (4) entity_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of entity types (e.g., 'class', 'function', 'file', 'directory') to include in the traversal. If None, all entity types are included.\n  (5) dependency_type_filter (['array', 'null'], optional): List of dependency types (e.g., 'contains', 'imports', 'invokes', 'inherits') to include in the traversal. If None, all dependency types are included.\n---- END FUNCTION #4 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #5: get_trace_artifacts ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC execution-trace artifacts for the current instance.\nThis tool reads `DaLIC/vt_results/<instance_id>/unique_artifacts.json` and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Call this once near the start of analyzing a new data point to inspect execution-trace evidence.\n- Call it again later with narrower `path_contains` or `name_contains` filters whenever you need a more precise slice.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) artifact_type (string, optional): Optional exact filter for artifact definition type.\nAllowed values: [`all`, `function`, `method`, `class`]\n  (2) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the compact file path.\n  (3) name_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to the qualified name.\n  (4) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset.\n  (5) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of artifacts to return in this slice.\n---- END FUNCTION #5 ----\n\n---- BEGIN FUNCTION #6: get_trace_data_dependencies ----\nDescription: \nRead a compact raw slice of DaLIC data-dependency evidence for the current instance.\nThis tool reads `DaLIC/vt_results/<instance_id>/unique_dep_tree.json` and returns a short JSON summary.\nIt does not rank or recommend results. It only applies exact filters and pagination.\n\nUsage suggestions:\n- Use it whenever you need a focused dependency slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- During analysis, you may call it again with narrower `symbol_contains`, `scope_contains`, `path_contains`, or `edge_type` filters.\n\nParameters:\n  (1) symbol_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol names.\n  (2) scope_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to symbol scopes.\n  (3) path_contains (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional case-insensitive substring filter applied to compact file paths.\n  (4) edge_type (['string', 'null'], optional): Optional exact filter for dependency edge type such as `data` or `ret`.\n  (5) start (integer, optional): Zero-based pagination offset for matched edges.\n  (6) limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of matched edges to return in this slice.\n  (7) path_limit (integer, optional): Maximum number of compact dependency paths to include.\n---- END FUNCTION #6 ----\n\n\nIf you choose to call a function ONLY reply in the following format with NO suffix:\n\n<function=example_function_name>\n<parameter=example_parameter_1>value_1</parameter>\n<parameter=example_parameter_2>\nThis is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\nmultiple lines\n</parameter>\n</function>\n\n<IMPORTANT>\nReminder:\n- Function calls MUST follow the specified format, start with <function= and end with </function>\n- Required parameters MUST be specified\n- Only call one function at a time\n- You may provide optional reasoning for your function call in natural language BEFORE the function call, but NOT after.\n- If there is no function call available, answer the question like normal with your current knowledge and do not tell the user about function calls\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "\nGiven the following GitHub issue description, your objective is to localize the specific files, classes or functions, and lines of code that are relevant to the solution for the issue. More specifically, these issues are all \"feature request\" issues, which means that you need to find the locations the feature described in the issue.\n\nFollow these steps to localize the issue:\n## Step 1: Categorize and Extract Key Problem Information\n - Classify the issue description into the following categories:\n    Requested feature description, behavior with out this feature, expected behavior with the feature, and additional context.\n - Identify modules in the 'scikit-learn' package mentioned in each category.\n - Use extracted keywords and line numbers to search for relevant code references for additional context.\n\n## Step 2: Locate Referenced Modules\n- Accurately determine specific modules\n    - Explore the repo to familiarize yourself with its structure.\n    - Analyze the described execution flow to identify specific modules or components being referenced.\n- Pay special attention to distinguishing between modules with similar names using context and described execution flow.\n- Output Format for collected relevant modules:\n    - Use the format: 'file_path:QualifiedName'\n    - E.g., for a function `calculate_sum` in the `MathUtils` class located in `src/helpers/math_helpers.py`, represent it as: 'src/helpers/math_helpers.py:MathUtils.calculate_sum'.\n\n## Step 3: Analyze the feature\n- Clarify the Purpose of the Issue\n    - If expanding capabilities: Identify where and how are the new behavior, fields, or modules incorporated.\n- Reconstruct the execution flow\n    - Identify main entry points triggering the feature described in the issue.\n    - Trace function calls, class interactions, and sequences of events.\n    - Identify potential relevant points in the code workflow of the codebase.\n    - If execution-trace or data-dependency tools are available, use them as compact hints to complement your understanding of the execution flow.\n    Important: Keep the reconstructed flow focused on the problem, avoiding irrelevant details.\n\n## Step 4: Locate Areas for Feature Implementation\n- Locate specific files, functions, or lines of code implementing the key part of the requested feature or containing critical information for resolving the issue.\n- Consider upstream and downstream dependencies that may affect or be affected by the issue.\n- Think Thoroughly: List multiple potential solutions and consider edge cases that could impact the resolution.\n\n## Output Format for Final Results:\nYour final output should list the locations requiring modification, wrapped with triple backticks ```\nEach location should include the file path, class name (if applicable), function name, or line numbers, ordered by importance.\nYour answer would better include about 5 files.\n\nIMPORTANT FORMAT RULES:\n- Output ONLY the location block, followed by `<finish></finish>`.\n- Do NOT include explanations, bullets, headings, numbering, summaries, or notes before or after the location block.\n- Each file path MUST appear on its own line and end with `.py`.\n- Do NOT write `file.py:QualifiedName` on a single line in the final answer.\n- After a file path line, use separate `line:`, `class:`, and `function:` lines as needed.\n\n### Examples:\n```\nfull_path1/file1.py\nline: 10\nclass: MyClass1\nfunction: my_function1\n\nfull_path2/file2.py\nline: 76\nfunction: MyClass2.my_function2\n\nfull_path3/file3.py\nline: 24\nline: 156\nfunction: my_function3\n```\n\nReturn just the location(s)\n\nNote: Your thinking should be thorough and so it's fine if it's very long.\n\n--- BEGIN PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\nTitle: MAINT conversion old->new/new->old tags\n\nTowards #30298 \r\n\r\nThis PR provides a way to convert old tags into new tags and new tags into old tags.\r\nWe should make sure that:\r\n\r\n- An estimator implementing `_get_tags` or `_more_tags` get a warning but that the check_estimator should be working reasonably\r\n- `_safe_tags` should raise a deprecation warning\r\n- we should make sure that `get_tags` can get old tags and convert it to new tags. The tricky part is to detect whether `__sklearn_tags__` if present is implemented by the `BaseEstimator` from scikit-learn or the child class. If this is only the `BaseEstimator`, we should raise a warning to move to the new API and we should temporary use the `_get_tags`/`_safe_tags`, otherwise we are fine.\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n\n\nSupplemental DaLIC evidence is available through tools for this instance.\n\nSuggested workflow:\n- At the beginning of analyzing a new data point, first inspect the raw execution-trace evidence with `get_trace_artifacts`.\n- During the analysis, you may call `get_trace_artifacts` again with narrower filters such as `path_contains` or `name_contains`.\n- When available, use `get_trace_data_dependencies` whenever you need a more precise dataflow slice around a traced symbol, scope, or file path.\n- You can revisit these tools at any point in the investigation; they are not only for the beginning.\n- Treat these tool outputs as compact raw evidence and verify them with the regular repository tools before finishing."}, {"content": "I'll help analyze this feature request systematically using the available tools.\n\nLet me start by exploring the repository structure to understand the codebase layout:\n\n<function=explore_tree_structure>\n<parameter=start_entities>['/']</parameter>\n<parameter=traversal_depth>2</parameter>\n<parameter=dependency_type_filter>['contains']</parameter>\n<parameter=entity_type_filter>['file', 'directory']</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "explore_tree_structure", "content": "OBSERVATION:\n/\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2500 .spin\n\u2502               \u2514\u2500\u2500 contains \u2500\u2