he horizontal baseline\n2645 |         is *left* (default 0).\n2646 | \n2647 |         Many parameters can take either a single value applying to all bars\n2648 |         or a sequence of values, one for each bar.\n2649 | \n2650 |         Parameters\n2651 |         ----------\n2652 |         y : float or array-like\n2653 |             The y coordinates of the bars. See also *align* for the\n2654 |             alignment of the bars to the coordinates.\n2655 | \n2656 |         width : float or array-like\n2657 |             The width(s) of the bars.\n2658 | \n2659 |             Note that if *left* has units (e.g. datetime), *width* should be in\n2660 |             units that are a difference from the value of *left* (e.g. timedelta).\n2661 | \n2662 |         height : float or array-like, default: 0.8\n2663 |             The heights of the bars.\n2664 | \n2665 |             Note that if *y* has units (e.g. datetime), then *height* should be in\n2666 |             units that are a difference (e.g. timedelta) around the *y* values.\n2667 | \n2668 |         left : float or array-like, default: 0\n2669 |             The x coordinates of the left side(s) of the bars.\n2670 | \n2671 |             Note that if *left* has units, then the x-axis will get a Locator and\n2672 |             Formatter appropriate for the units (e.g. dates, or categorical).\n2673 | \n2674 |         align : {'center', 'edge'}, default: 'center'\n2675 |             Alignment of the base to the *y* coordinates*:\n2676 | \n2677 |             - 'center': Center the bars on the *y* positions.\n2678 |             - 'edge': Align the bottom edges of the bars with the *y*\n2679 |               positions.\n2680 | \n2681 |             To align the bars on the top edge pass a negative *height* and\n2682 |             ``align='edge'``.\n2683 | \n2684 |         Returns\n2685 |         -------\n2686 |         `.BarContainer`\n2687 |             Container with all the bars and optionally errorbars.\n2688 | \n2689 |         Other Parameters\n2690 |         ----------------\n2691 |         color : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, optional\n2692 |             The colors of the bar faces.\n2693 | \n2694 |         edgecolor : :mpltype:`color` or list of :mpltype:`color`, optional\n2695 |             The colors of the bar edges.\n2696 | \n2697 |         linewidth : float or array-like, optional\n2698 |             Width of the bar edge(s). If 0, don't draw edges.\n2699 | \n2700 |         tick_label : str or list of str, optional\n2701 |             The tick labels of the bars.\n2702 |             Default: None (Use default numeric labels.)\n2703 | \n2704 |         label : str or list of str, optional\n2705 |             A single label is attached to the resulting `.BarContainer` as a\n2706 |             label for the whole dataset.\n2707 |             If a list is provided, it must be the same length as *y* and\n2708 |             labels the individual bars. Repeated labels are not de-duplicated\n2709 |             and will cause repeated label entries, so this is best used when\n2710 |             bars also differ in style (e.g., by passing a list to *color*.)\n2711 | \n2712 |         xerr, yerr : float or array-like of shape(N,) or shape(2, N), optional\n2713 |             If not *None*, add horizontal / vertical errorbars to the bar tips.\n2714 |             The values are +/- sizes relative to the data:\n2715 | \n2716 |             - scalar: symmetric +/- values for all bars\n2717 |             - shape(N,): symmetric +/- values for each bar\n2718 |             - shape(2, N): Separate - and + values for each bar. First row\n2719 |               contains the lower errors, the second row contains the upper\n2720 |               errors.\n2721 |             - *None*: No errorbar. (default)\n2722 | \n2723 |             See :doc:`/gallery/statistics/errorbar_features` for an example on\n2724 |             the usage of *xerr* and *yerr*.\n2725 | \n2726 |         ecolor : :